/** Siman 092 translations — part 3 */
export const TRANSLATIONS_P3 = {
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': ["A gentile tastes. See below siman 98 that we require specifically kafeilah or mislaleta — see what is written there."],
    '1#ב': ["When is this said — when he preceded, etc. For then there is only on account of the meat taste itself; and since a gentile tasted and said there is no taste, it is permitted; but if he did not manage [in time], etc. — then it is possible the milk itself absorbed in the meat and the piece became nevelah, went out and mixed in the rest of the milk, and cannot be tasted — for milk in milk it is; therefore one needs sixty in the milk against the whole piece, for we do not know how much was absorbed and discharged from it."],
    '2#א': ["Milk fell, etc. Through the end of the seif is Rambam's language ch. 9 of Ma'achalot Asurot. The explanation: when it fell on one piece, the rest in the pot do not join to nullify the milk but there must be sixty in the piece itself to nullify the taste; when there is not sixty in the piece the piece becomes nevelah; when he stirs afterward and the piece enters among the rest of the pot it forbids again what is in the pot until sixty against the piece — but if he stirred immediately at the time the drop fell the piece immediately enters the whole pot and the whole pot joins and afterward one is not forbidden unless there is not sixty in the pot and piece against the milk, and same with covering. That which Tur wrote below siman 105 seif 4 — if wholly outside the gravy it is only kedei netilah for R' Yehuda, and for Rashi even if part is in the gravy — perhaps milk differs as a fat issur since there is greasiness of butter) and it absorbs in the whole piece as in the Gemara ch. Kol HaBasar, and agreed by all poskim below siman 105 seif 5 that a fat issur steams through the whole piece — so answered in Darkei Moshe siman 105; and that which Taz wrote siman 87 seif 10 that blood dripping on roast by the fire forbids only kedei netilah — end — because blood is not a fat issur as proven in several places. Or one may distinguish as Ran ch. 25 wrote, etc.: this piece since it stands in the pot, from the steam and moisture of the milk it spreads through all of it even outside the gravy — end of his words — see there; and below siman 105 although it also deals with a pot, one may say it deals with a thick issur that is not clear and certainly does not spread through the whole piece except through gravy — so divided Maharshal, and Perishah brings him; and the first distinction is hard from Rashba in Torat HaBayit (daf 99b) and Ran that milk is not considered a fat issur — see there; and so from Taz's words below siman 105 seif 7 they ruled that a weak issur does not steam from piece to piece without gravy and a fat issur steams as Rashba — and Rashba learns this from milk here that does not steam from piece to piece without gravy — but requires study; one may say it deals that the meat piece is fatty as Taz wrote siman 105 seif 5; and I expanded on this in my book and challenged Bach on the Rav there in siman 105 seif 5 — see there; here I shortened since we always estimate at sixty as below siman 105 — see there."],
    '2#ב': ["That piece was forbidden and they estimate, etc. The poskim and Tur brought the dispute of Rashi and R' Yehuda: R' Yehuda holds only when the whole piece is outside the gravy, such as the piece beneath has part outside the gravy and this one rests on it; but if part is in the gravy, boiling mixes the taste in the whole pot and everything joins to nullify the milk. Rashi explains even if part is in the gravy not everything joins to nullify the milk. The view of all authors (except Rashba and R' Yerucham, brought in Bedek HaBayit) is like R' Yehuda, and so all Acharonim; and so Maharshal in his glosses to Tur, and Perishah brings him — except in his book ch. Kol HaBasar siman 61 he did not rule thus."],
    '2#ג': ["But if he stirred from beginning to end, etc. This is Rambam's language, and Tur wrote on it: it appears one need only stir or cover immediately at the time of falling, and even if he ceased stirring afterward — end of his words; and R' Yehuda ibn Chabib wrote that Rambam's view is also thus, and that which he mentioned stirring from beginning to end was because at the outset he had to mention stirring at the end — for otherwise why would everything in the pot be forbidden — and therefore he also mentioned stirring at the end in the conclusion — end of his words; and per this the Mechaber should not have been silent and should have explained that stirring at the beginning alone suffices; but he followed his view written in Beit Yosef that Rambam requires stirring at beginning and end, and the Rav in the gloss that stirring at the beginning alone suffices; and this is what he wrote: and likewise if he stirred at the beginning, etc.; and he did not write this law in language of \"some say\" because the Mechaber's words can also be explained thus, as Mahari ibn Chabib explained Rambam's words; but the Mechaber's own view is not so, as I wrote; nevertheless the view of Mahari ibn Chabib and the Rav is primary, and so from Rashi (daf 109a) — see there; and so Maharshal ch. 25 siman 45 and Acharonim; and it appears Tur did not intend to challenge but to explain Rambam's words."],
    '2#ד': ["And likewise if he did not stir, etc. — if there is sixty in the pot against the drop of milk, etc. One might say that when there is not sixty in the pot it should also be permitted since he did not stir at all and it only imparted taste in that piece itself, as in the Gemara and poskim, and explicitly in Raavad's challenges ch. 9 of Ma'achalot Asurot — see there; and Darkei Moshe brings it briefly; and it appears what requires sixty in the pot is when part is in the gravy, for the Rav is in doubt whether we hold like R' Yehuda who mixes taste in all or like Rashi — therefore the pot is permitted either way and the piece is forbidden lest he does not mix taste in all and the pot does not join (and nevertheless what was written in Shulchan Aruch and Hagahot Maimoniyot in name of Rambam is a mistake and should read: and nevertheless in name of Rambam)."],
    '2#ה': ["And the rest of the pot is permitted. See below siman 105 seif 7 s.k. 16 that nevertheless all other pieces that touched this piece need netilah."],
    '2#ו': ["And likewise if it fell, etc. This is Rambam's language, and Tur wrote: I do not understand his words — if the pieces are all in the gravy why need to stir, and if not in the gravy what does stirring help if he stirs afterward — end of his words; and the Rav in the gloss: and some dispute, etc.; and although Beit Yosef brought in name of Mahari ibn Chabib that Rambam also holds thus, and what he wrote \"he stirs the whole pot\" is not that he stirs afterward but comes to teach that when it is known on which it fell he stirs only until that piece is mixed among the pieces; and when not known, immediately when he saw the drop fall into the pot and did not pay attention to which piece it fell, he should hurry to stir or cover immediately so the piece will mix — nevertheless the Rav did not refrain from writing in language of \"and some dispute\" since the plain meaning does not imply this, although Tur also did not understand thus and likewise understood Rambam's words like Tur and the Rav; and this is primary in Rambam's words, as explained, and Bach explained Rambam's words like Mahari ibn Chabib's explanation (except he unnecessarily attributed to Rambam that he holds like R' Yehuda); and he wrote on the Rav: may his Master forgive him — and per my humble opinion the Rav's view is correct, as I wrote; but what Beit Yosef wrote that Rambam deals when the pieces are in the gravy and nevertheless stirring is required to mix well — is hard: if so what is \"and likewise,\" and further this law comes to teach even when known on which piece it fell; and further gravy certainly mixes all pieces — why stir; and one should not challenge Mahari ibn Chabib on falling into gravy — one may say it deals when there is not sixty in the gravy itself but in combining the pieces he stirs them in the gravy; requires study. Or the question is: and likewise if it fell into the pot or into the pieces or into the gravy and it is not known on which piece it fell or whether it fell into the gravy — and he mentioned gravy generally because it matters for two pots — requires study. However the law is tangled per Mahari ibn Chabib's explanation; therefore the words are as their plain meaning, and Rambam holds that since it is not known on which piece it fell we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah but the whole pot enters doubt and therefore he stirs the whole pot and the whole pot joins to nullify — similar to what Rashba and Tur wrote siman 111: there were two pots and issur fell into one and he does not know which — and neither alone can nullify it but both join to nullify since whatever will mix in the future is viewed as already mixed — end of his words; and this is similar here; and so Orach Chaim general rule 29 din 4: where the issur is not clarified we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah. I later found Orach Chaim wrote similarly on Rambam's words: and what he wrote \"and likewise if it fell,\" etc. — because since it is not known which piece it is, one cannot say chatichah na'asah nevelah on it and cannot forbid everything since there is no noten ta'am in milk — end of his words; clearly as I wrote; and this is clear Rambam's view per my humble opinion; and one should not challenge how can he stir the whole pot lest there be taste in one piece and how nullify issur l'chatchila — one may say since it is not known on which it fell the whole pot enters doubt and there is no issue of nullifying issur; and it is like what Maharai wrote in Torat HaEsh siman 171: this is not nullifying issur l'chatchila since it is doubtful whether any issur will mix at all — end of his words; I brought this in siman 84 s.k. 38 and below siman 196 s.k. 28; and here too it is doubtful whether there is taste in this piece; and it appears Tur also understood Rambam thus — but holds since it can be established through gentile tasting each piece, if so not everything enters doubt because tasting each piece will clarify — and since one piece would certainly be clarified as forbidden, what does stirring afterward help; and per Rashba's view one cannot clarify which pot it fell into — Rashba certainly deals only in min b'mino where one cannot establish through taste, as he brought this law in Torat HaBayit from terumah where the measure is a kav in min b'mino — see there."],
    '3#_': ["And if he recognizes it he throws it away and the others are permitted. When there is sixty against the piece; and if he does not recognize it the gravy is permitted when there is sixty in the pieces and gravy, and all the pieces are forbidden if the issur piece is fit to be honored — for we hold below siman 101 that in basar b'chalav it is issur from the substance itself; and if it is fit to be honored it is not nullified in a thousand; and if it is not fit to be honored it is nullified in majority as below siman 109 — see there."],
    '4#א': ["They did not say chatichah na'asah nevelah except in basar b'chalav, etc. The reason poskim wrote: meat and milk are each permitted by itself, and when together they are forbidden — therefore the meat itself becomes issur; not so in other issurim."],
    '4#ב': ["And then even the piece itself returns to being permitted, etc. One might say below siman 106 he wrote the piece itself remains in its issur because the issur in it is not fully discharged — end of his words; and the Rav in the gloss there understood these contradict each other; to avoid contradiction he explains explicitly: what he wrote here means per what he wrote earlier in basar b'chalav that even though the gravy is permitted with sixty, nevertheless the piece remains in issur; if it is fit to be honored and he does not recognize it, it is not nullified in a thousand — and he teaches that it is not so in other issurim but nullified in majority when not recognized since there is no issur from the substance itself, as below siman 101; and then even the piece itself returns to being permitted and permitted to eat all of it and need not throw one away as below siman 109."],
    '4#ג': ["And some say we say in all issurim chatichah na'asah nevelah, etc. Agur in name of Maharai wrote: nevertheless for all it is only rabbinic; Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe bring it; and so Ran that in other issurim it is rabbinic — I brought this in siman 22 s.k. 10 — see there."],
    '4#ד': ["But if part of the piece is in the gravy, etc., until but in basar b'chalav, etc., we say chatichah na'asah nevelah. The Rav ruled thus — all follows his reasoning like a puzzle — who like the wise knows its interpretation; but in truth it requires study in this matter regarding part outside the gravy, for what was written in Shulchan Aruch from Orach Chaim general rule 28 that even in other issurim and part in the gravy one should forbid that piece itself — is not so; on the contrary Orach Chaim wrote there explicitly several times that even in basar b'chalav if part is in the gravy the whole pot joins to nullify the issur and even the piece itself is permitted — see there; and this is ruling like R' Yehuda as I brought in s.k. 4, and Darkei Moshe brings it explicitly there in the conclusion to rule the law; and what the Rav wrote that in basar b'chalav even if part is in the gravy chatichah na'asah nevelah — also requires study, for it is against all poskim, for in the Gemara ch. 25 (daf 108–109) we say a drop of milk on a meat piece — the piece is forbidden and does not forbid all other pieces (until sixty against it) except through stirring and covering; and R' Yehuda and Rabbenu Tam explained: wholly outside the gravy; but if part is in the gravy the whole pot joins to nullify and then even the piece is permitted — and so ruled Semag (negative commandment 141 daf 51a), Semak siman 213 daf 101a, Sha'arim siman 95, Mordechai, Agudah ch. Kol HaBasar, Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit daf 99, Maharil, and other authors like R' Yehuda; and even for Rashi who explained when part is in the gravy it appears no chatichah na'asah nevelah to forbid the others except through stirring and covering; and it appears what the Rav wrote that in basar b'chalav even if part is in the gravy chatichah na'asah nevelah — this is when he stirred afterward until all entered the gravy, and not that we say from the start the whole pot joins to nullify because part is in the gravy as in other issurim; for at the outset, either the whole piece is outside the gravy and issur fell on it, etc. — it must deal with stirring; for if not, chatichah na'asah nevelah would not forbid the rest of the pot — therefore the conclusion also deals with this; and regarding what he wrote in other issurim nevertheless to be stringent to forbid that piece — it appears this is the Rav's own view, for he is in doubt whether we hold like Rashi or R' Yehuda, as I wrote in s.k. 7; but regarding the rest of the pot he did not want to be stringent since otherwise some say we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah except in basar b'chalav — requires study; and nevertheless it is already explained in s.k. 4 that the view of all authors and Acharonim is like R' Yehuda — see there."],
    '4#ה': ["Some say we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah if wet issur was mixed with wet heter and afterward everything was mixed with other heter, etc. Meaning: specifically when wet issur mixed with dry heter we say chatichah na'asah nevelah from importance, for the pieces of issur are recognizable and distinct — chatichah na'asah nevelah; but not when wet issur mixed with wet heter such as yayin nesech with kosher wine, or milk with fat, and afterward everything mixed with other heter — whether the other heter is wet or dry — requires study; and see Yeshu'ot Yaakov ch. 3 siman 60."],
    '4#ו': ["But not in basar b'chalav, etc. The language of Ikkarim (4:53a): however in basar b'chalav one should be stringent even in wet — end of his words; and per my humble opinion this is not stringency but that all agree here, as proven explicitly in the Gemara ch. 25 (daf 108): there they say: why is milk permitted — it is nevelah milk; for in basar b'chalav even in wet chatichah na'asah nevelah; and so Rashba in Torat HaBayit daf 98a — see there."],
    '4#ז': ["We do not say in any issur chatichah na'asah nevelah. Meaning in both basar b'chalav and other issurim — rather whenever more heter is added afterward it is as if mixed from the start, and its law is as below siman 109; and see below siman 99 s.k. 5 and whether we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in salting — Peri Chadash ruled: even though they practice to estimate all salting at sixty, nevertheless since many poskim hold all salting only forbids kedei kezayit, one may permit for great loss or for a poor person in an important matter and say it is not chatichah na'asah nevelah — end of his words; and it appears there that even in a fatty matter that forbids until sixty as below siman 105 we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah — and so appears primary, as I wrote in my book, and not like Be'er Sheva daf 82b; and see siman 22 s.k. 10 whether we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in kelipah — see there; and see below siman 134 s.k. 15 more on chatichah na'asah nevelah."],
    '4#ח': ["A vessel forbidden through absorption of issur, etc. See the following seif and what is written there, and siman 98 s.k. 5, and what is written there."],
    '5#א': ["A drop of milk, etc. So wrote Semak: a drop that fell on the vessel wall — we are in doubt in ch. Dam Chatat whether it steams through the whole vessel or not; therefore when it fell opposite the gravy and there is sixty in the gravy from the drop it is permitted either way — if it steams through the whole vessel it is nullified, and if it does not steam then it should not forbid (meaning if it does not steam through the whole vessel it should not forbid because presumably it steams inward and is as if it fell in the food — so Beit Yosef explains); but when it fell in the empty place, perhaps it steams on the pot wall until near the gravy so there is not sixty from the drop, and that part of the vessel becomes nevelah, and when he pours the food, etc. — end of his words; and per these words they are the Mechaber's words, only abbreviated — and see siman 121 s.k. 17."],
    '5#ב': ["Only sixty against the drop. Meaning to permit the food, but the pot is nevertheless forbidden and he should pour the food immediately on the other side — as the Rav wrote in seif 6, and so Terumat HaChacham beginning general rule 56."],
    '5#ג': ["Then the food becomes forbidden. It deals when there is not sixty in the food against that empty place — namely sixty times sixty-one slightly less against the drop, for the drop is forbidden until sixty; therefore with the drop there is sixty-one slightly less. It appears poskim who wrote sixty of sixty drops like it suffices is not precise — requires study; and it appears one may not pour the food through that place of the pot, for one may not nullify issur l'chatchila; and even in a new pot it appears forbidden to pour lest perhaps it only steams in part of the vessel and was not nullified at first and now when pouring it will be nullified. What the Rav wrote in the gloss that in a new pot in every case one needs only sixty against the drop — meaning if he poured b'dieved suffices with sixty against the drop alone; but l'chatchila he may not pour — and this is not as he wrote \"but if it is a new pot in every case permitted to pour\" — for he refers to the Mechaber's words; but certainly as I wrote."],
    '5#ד': ["And he should not touch it until it cools. But piercing the vessel from below does not help — we fear perhaps the gravy will touch the wall forbidden through shaking — so Semak and poskim; and it appears also that pouring immediately on the other side does not help lest the pot shake — so Semak and poskim there, and Orach Chaim, and Maharshal in his book siman 37."],
    '5#ה': ["But if it is new, etc. Per the Rav's gloss siman 98 seif 5 one might be stringent to say even a new earthenware vessel chatichah na'asah nevelah — therefore here specifically for a drop of milk we say thus because all is still permitted; not for a drop of issur; but it is already explained siman 98 s.k. 20 that we cannot say the earthenware body becomes nevelah; if so here even for a drop of issur the law is thus; but in an old pot what is absorbed is chatichah na'asah nevelah — specifically an old pot beit yomo, for otherwise what is in the pot walls does not become nevelah since noten ta'am li'fegam, as below siman 98 — requires study."],
    '5#ו': ["As below siman 94 regarding the ladle. And even though it appears no distinction for the ladle — since it is new and he stirred afterward in the pot he should need only against what it absorbed — but here the pot is considered like old since they now cook meat in it and milk fell during cooking — nevertheless since you come to forbid the pot wall due to the drop, one must say perhaps cooking does not steam through the whole vessel and then the food above did not steam and if it steamed above the drop also spreads below; or say it deals with a new pot where the drop fell before the food was hot, or after the drop fell on a pot of water they put meat in it — requires study."],
    '6#א': ["People practice, etc. Maharai who was last and great in his generation investigated all customs and wrote they practice to permit through cooling as in Semak, and Hagahot Sh\"D siman 55, and so Maharshal in Orach Chaim and his book there."],
    '6#ב': ["When it fell on the wall, etc. It appears specifically in an old pot they practice to forbid when it fell not opposite the gravy, for the reason Orach Chaim general rule 31 din 3 wrote: we fear perhaps afterward once the pot boiled it touched that part of the wall that was forbidden — end of his words; but in a new pot certainly if there is sixty in the food against the drop they did not practice to forbid, for even if it touches there is no issur here; and likewise in an old pot that is not beit yomo; and likewise in an old pot beit yomo with sixty times sixty-one slightly less against the drop — in all this they did not practice issur and it is permitted for all, nevertheless wait until it cools."],
    '6#ג': ["And then the pot is also permitted. And in Terumat HaChacham there he wrote in din 3 one should be stringent l'chatchila per Orach Chaim to forbid the pot — see there."],
    '6#ד': ["And then the pot is forbidden, etc. Because we fear perhaps it did not steam all inward but something remained in the pot wall; and one should not challenge: perhaps something remained in the pot wall and spread in width until there is not sixty in the wall against it — then need sixty in the food against that wall that was forbidden; if it spread in width it spread in all until sixty, and if it did not spread in the whole vessel presumably it entered inward and was nullified — therefore the food is permitted; but regarding the pot we fear perhaps a little remained in thickness that did not enter inward and it is forbidden; therefore if he cooked another dish in it its law is like the first time and sixty suffices if it fell opposite the gravy like the first time, for there is only concern lest a little remained — and certainly not more than a drop; nevertheless since we do not know how much remained, in the second time need sixty against the whole drop — requires study; and see Orach Chaim."],
    '6#ה': ["And he should pour immediately. On the other side and not leave it to cool — thus after it fell opposite the gravy we fear lest it steam more from here onward — so Orach Chaim."],
    '7#א': ["Such as erev Shabbat, etc. And the same law for any great need such as for guests, great loss, or a poor person per the teacher's judgment — so Terumat HaChacham beginning general rule 56."],
    '7#ב': ["Even not opposite the gravy. And then wait until it cools as in seif 6, and so he wrote there; and this is if possible; but if needed immediately for guests and the like, he need not wait until cool but should pour on the other side — so Maharshal wrote there."],
    '7#ג': ["Hot pot, etc. Excluding cold, for then even the pot is permitted."],
    '7#ד': ["Not by the fire. For if it is by the fire and it is hot, clearly it is like kli rishon — all the more so; and even if he placed a cold pot on it, everything is forbidden, even the food inside."],
    '7#ה': ["And the food is permitted. Maharshal wrote in Orach Chaim and his book ch. 25 siman 38: if the pot is boiling from the fire's heat, even the food is forbidden if there is not sixty against what is beneath it; and it appears from Maharshal's words there that even if the spill is cold it is forbidden since the pot boils from the fire's heat — see there; and see siman 98 and what I wrote there."],
    '7#ו': ["It is like iruy. Meaning we do not say it cooled because it flowed on the stove — and so Terumat HaDeshen there; however this is specifically when yad soledes bo applies there — and so Terumat HaDeshen there, and it is simple."],
    '7#ז': ["For iruy forbids only kedei kelipah. Therefore even if there is food in the pot where kelipah remains, one need not peel — the pot is like kelipah; and so Maharshal there."],
    '7#ח': ["For the lower prevails. And no distinction to what is written below siman 105 seif 3 in the gloss that if permitted and issur rest together and one is cold, kelipah suffices — here since the stream comes from above it is like the upper; or since the pot stands in place it is considered lower; or here it splashes on the pot as Bach end siman 91 — see Perishah ch. 25 siman 62: specifically when it splashes on the pot; but if milk dripped under the meat pot the milk has the law of lower and does not forbid the food in the pot — but per Rashal and Shach below, if the pot boils from the fire even cold spill forbids the food until sixty — see there; therefore even if milk dripped under the pot it also forbids the food; and per Taz's words if the stove place is hot yad soledes bo the milk is like kli rishon and forbids the food too — requires study."],
    '7#ט': ["When the pot began to boil. But if it did not begin to boil, what is in the pot is not forbidden at all even without sixty, for it is still like cold and does not steam into what is in the pot — end of Terumat HaChacham there din 4."],
    '9#_': ["But milk of yours, etc. Meaning a candle called kreuzel that burns from rendered-fat milk and is boiling from the fire; but milk of yours boiling in a pot that the pot boiled by the fire — scraping suffices, for it is not considered iruy since the stream broke as above seif 7 and siman 98 seif 10 in the gloss; and so in Terumat HaDeshen there."],
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `A kezayit of meat, etc. There are here two prohibitions: one from the side of meat taste that the meat discharges to the milk immediately after the falling there — and therefore sixty in the milk helps according to all; and there is another prohibition, namely the milk that the meat absorbs into itself returns and forbids the rest of the milk in the cauldron — and in this sixty does not help according to Rashi who holds min b'mino in mashahu; and this prohibition applies only after the cauldron rests, then the meat returns and discharges the milk it absorbed, and not before; but we hold like Rabbenu Tam that even min b'mino is nullified at sixty — therefore also after the cauldron rests nevertheless sixty helps; only Rambam, who is the holder of this view, holds that even with less than sixty there is permission through tasting of kafeilah — and therefore if he did not precede and remove it, sixty is required because then the prohibition comes from the milk that became forbidden inside the meat and entered the rest of the milk, and the taste cannot be sensed; and we do not hold thus, but in every case we require sixty against the meat and then it is permitted; and the kezayit mentioned here is not precise, but the same applies to less than a kezayit — only regarding lashes there is a difference.`,
    '2#א': `Into a pot of meat. This deals when the drop fell on one piece outside the gravy — for if inside the gravy certainly everything in the pot joins to nullify the drop; and Tur wrote the dispute of Rashi and R' Yehuda: R' Yehuda holds only when the whole piece is outside the gravy, such as one piece has part outside the gravy and this rests on it — therefore that piece alone is forbidden; but if part is in the gravy, boiling mixes the whole pot and everything joins to nullify the milk; and Rashi holds even if part is in the gravy not everything joins to nullify the milk; and there is a leniency for Rashi on one side — namely if part is in the gravy and this piece is forbidden it does not forbid the rest, as Darkei Moshe wrote; and it does not appear to my humble opinion that although the gravy below does not help nullify the drop of milk that fell since boiling does not reach there, nevertheless that piece itself is forbidden — the lower part from the upper part — and returns and forbids all the gravy and what is in it until sixty against the whole piece, for the whole piece becomes nevelah since part of the piece rests in the gravy the gravy conveys to the rest; and from these words of Shulchan Aruch which are Rambam's words it appears to me he holds like Rashi since he wrote if the piece was forbidden it forbids the rest — therefore it appears to me this is how we hold, although Maharshal in his Orach Chaim ruled like R' Yehuda, nevertheless in his book ch. 25 siman 49 he was stringent like Rashi.`,
    '2#ב': `That piece was forbidden. Terumat HaChacham general rule 22 wrote that Tur's words here contradict what he wrote in siman 105 seif 4 that if issur fell on the piece in the pot it only forbids kedei netilah; and Maharshal resolved that here it deals with a clear issur and there with a thick issur that does not spread as much. And per what I wrote in siman 87 seif 10 that according to Rosh and Tur milk is a fat issur and steams here too — there is no difficulty, for it is explained in siman 105 the distinction between weak and fat issur; and so I found afterward in Darkei Moshe siman 105 that he resolved thus, and wrote this is not like Rashba that Beit Yosef brings there that milk does not steam — but in siman 105 seif 7 it is proven there that milk does not steam — requires study.`,
    '2#ג': `But if he stirred from beginning to end. Not precise — rather if he stirred immediately it suffices, as Tur wrote explicitly; and Mahari ibn Chabib challenged that for R' Yehuda one need not stir but only that part be in the gravy; and it appears to me that here too it suffices, only since the pot is full and one cannot well insert the piece that rests outside on another piece that has part in the gravy, as I wrote above for R' Yehuda — therefore one must stir the pot so the upper become lower; and it is already explained that we hold like Rashi.`,
    '2#ד': `And likewise if he did not stir at all. These matters require study — behold they are Raavad's view in Beit Yosef, and these are his words: if he did not stir at all the other pieces are not forbidden, although if he did not stir gravy goes out and becomes nevelah from that piece to another piece — nevertheless they are not forbidden, for the milk must go out to the other pieces — end of his words; and if so it is hard why Rama wrote that sixty is required in the pot against the milk, for the milk does not go out to the other pieces at all; and if it does go out, behold it forbids and one would need sixty in the pot against the whole forbidden piece — requires study. And in Orach Chaim general rule 28 he also wrote to permit when there is sixty in the whole pot against the drop that fell on a piece that has part outside the gravy, because he holds like R' Yehuda — only he wrote that then the piece also returns to permission, unlike Rama here; and one must say Rama is stringent from his own reasoning to forbid that piece even per R' Yehuda, and so it appears from his words in seif 4 that he rules like R' Yehuda where part is outside the gravy, for leniency; and one might say Rama should have written this here in language of "some say," for Rambam who is the first view holds like Rashi that sixty is required against the whole piece, as I wrote below; and Rama's language implies he did not come to dispute but to add; and further it is hard that in seif 4 Rama concludes in basar b'chalav one should be stringent and here he ruled leniently, and it deals with basar b'chalav.`,
    '2#ה': `Although he did not stir and covered at the end, etc. Tur wrote this disputing Rambam and holds thus; and Beit Yosef wrote Rambam's reason from what we say in the Gemara "from this we learn R' Yehuda holds," etc. — it appears to Rambam that only in such a case is it permitted, but if he stirred at the beginning and not at the end, no — lest he did not stir well — requires study on this; for why do we say in truth in the Gemara "from this we learn R' Yehuda holds even if he stirred from beginning to end," etc. — for we have no proof on this except from what Rabbi said "the words of R' Yehuda appear correct without stirring but only at the end" — from this we learn R' Yehuda is more stringent; whence do you know he is so stringent even with stirring from beginning to end — perhaps he is stringent only with stirring immediately alone, which is one degree more than not stirring except at the end; but whence do you say he is stringent with stirring from beginning to end which is two degrees? Granted for Tur, stirring from beginning to end is one matter with stirring at the beginning alone — only he challenged what Tur saw to prove from Rambam's words more than from the Gemara's words, for there too they said the language "the words of the Sages appear correct with stirring from beginning to end" — therefore it is stirring immediately alone per Tur's view; and if so what is Tur's difficulty in Rambam's words more than the Gemara's? And it appears Tur holds one must infer in the Gemara: from "the words of R' Yehuda appear correct without stirring but only at the end" it implies with stirring at the beginning alone it is permitted; and afterward they said "the words of the Sages appear correct with stirring from beginning to end" — it implies stirring at the beginning alone does not help; and therefore one of them is not precise; and we find similar in Rosh that Beit Yosef brings in siman 95 that we go stringently; nevertheless here too one cannot infer to say an excess, for the reverse could also be asked; rather no proof applies here; therefore Rambam ruled stringently, namely that he mentioned permission only with stirring from beginning to end, although in prohibition he mentioned only stirring at the end alone — nevertheless he relied that we will explain stringently; and on this Tur disputes and holds the inference of the first part is primary, since for Rav we are not concerned lest he did not stir well — therefore with stirring at the beginning alone suffices, as Tur intended; but in truth one may resolve the Gemara's and Rambam's words so there is no dispute between Rambam and Tur in law, only in explaining the language that Tur explains "from beginning to end" means he did not cease stirring until he removed it from the fire — therefore he disputes Rambam per his reasoning; but Rambam explains otherwise: as long as there is substance of the drop of milk on the meat, what he stirred in the pot does not help, lest that part returned above afterward and again forbids that piece by itself since it still exists visibly on it — therefore we say he should stir until the substance of the drop ceases, and this is "from beginning to end" in the Gemara said regarding the end of the drop's existence; and so is Rambam's intent; but if the substance of the drop finished, Rambam concedes to Tur that it suffices if he afterward ceases to stir — as I wrote, correct. And in what Tur wrote afterward "and I do not understand his words," etc. — per Tur's understanding of Rambam's words that it deals after he waited a bit he stirred the pot — therefore he challenged what stirring helps, etc.; but Beit Yosef and Mahari ibn Chabib explained Rambam's words that it deals when he did not wait at all — and whence does Tur have to explain Rambam's words that it deals when he waited a bit and to raise his difficulty? One may say he proved thus from that he wrote "he stirs the pot so it will mix" — future tense — and did not say "if he stirred" as in the beginning, but certainly that here it deals that they instruct him thus if he comes to ask — so is Tur's intent; but per my humble opinion Rambam's words are not explained at all in this way; and at first I also inferred in his words "one may not nullify issur l'chatchila," and Mahari ibn Chabib thought to correct this and did not correct, for even if he did not wait and stirred immediately, nevertheless Rambam's language implies l'chatchila he should do so — and how can he insert the piece that has milk on it into the gravy and nullify the milk l'chatchila; rather the matter is clear that here Rambam deals regarding that in truth everything joins, namely it fell into the gravy; and likewise if it fell on a piece that rests outside the gravy only it is not known which it is — therefore we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, as the Rav HaMaggid wrote: since he does not know which piece it is, one cannot say chatichah na'asah nevelah on it and cannot forbid everything since there is no noten ta'am in milk — end of his words. And Rambam comes here to say in what matter the joining of other pieces helps: if there is a gentile kafeilah who can taste whether there is milk taste, and if he does not taste milk taste it is permitted even though there is no sixty there, as Beit Yosef wrote in name of Rambam in siman 98 — therefore he said here if there is a gentile to taste, then he stirs everything so it mixes and afterward gives him to taste; for if he gives him to taste immediately, he will certainly taste milk taste from what floated above; and in truth if he mixes and gives him to taste he will not taste milk taste because the milk was nullified in the mixture; and we only declare forbidden if the gentile tastes milk taste after it is mixed well — and it turns out there will be loss in vain if he does not mix; therefore he said stir so everything mixes if there is milk taste in the whole pot — meaning not in what floated above alone, for then specifically it is forbidden; and likewise in a piece that is not known which it is; and the law is that other pieces also join with it, and if he gives one piece to taste it is possible he will taste that piece on which it fell and taste milk taste and from this forbid everything; and in truth if he mixes that milk taste will be nullified — therefore he stirs everything. And if so he properly said afterward: immediately if a gentile is not found to taste and we rely on him, they estimate at sixty, etc. — meaning in permission through gentile tasting he must stir first for the reason we said lest there be loss; but when there is no gentile and permission is through sixty, then in truth he need not stir at all since everything joins to sixty; and now there is no nullifying issur l'chatchila, for in any case it is mixed — only the stirring is for the owners' benefit; and all is well with heaven's help that He illuminates our eyes from heaven; and although our teacher Tur did not explain this in Rambam's words, we explain in Rambam's words.`,
    '3#א': `And if he cooked it with others, etc. But without cooking it does not forbid even if both are hot; and we did not say in siman 91 seif 4 hot into hot that forbids everything, except for issur from the substance itself — not so here with absorbed issur; and although in siman 105 seif 6 he equated basar b'chalav to issur from the substance itself, that is meat itself with milk itself, as explained there in our words in the section.`,
    '3#ב': `If the issur piece is fit to be honored. Explained in siman 101.`,
    '4#א': `Except in basar b'chalav. The reason: each is permitted by itself, and when together they are forbidden — therefore the meat becomes issur, and one is lashed if he eats half a kezayit of meat and half a kezayit of milk; therefore sixty is required in the gravy to nullify the whole piece on which a drop of milk fell — so Tosafot, and explanation of their words: since in basar b'chalav there is no recognizable issur except after mixture, therefore the whole mixture is the substance of the issur that is the prohibition stated in Torah "mixing meat in milk"; not so in another thing that is issur by itself and that is the Torah prohibition, only here it mixed incidentally — you have only a Torah doubt, and chatichah na'asah nevelah does not apply — so Maggid Mishneh.`,
    '4#ב': `And then even the piece itself, etc. It appears thus per what Tur wrote that we do not say "possible to squeeze out" forbidden in other issurim — automatically "possible to squeeze out" permitted and the piece itself is permitted; and see below siman 107 seif 1 he wrote the opposite, and that is the truth.`,
    '4#ג': `And some say we say in all issurim, etc. And this is rabbinic — therefore one may lean to permission in a place I will mention below in name of Maharshal; and it is explained in Orach Chaim general rule 24 that even in rabbinic issur we say chatichah na'asah nevelah — and this is like Rashba that I mentioned in siman 90 seif 1.`,
    '4#ד': `If the issur is attached, etc. In Orach Chaim he explains the reason in name of Semak: since it is attached to this issur, and likewise a worm in its body, and likewise a kosher fish found in its innards after cooking with a sheretz or non-kosher fish and there was not sixty in the fish itself against it — in all such cases we fear perhaps once it remained alone in the gravy at the end of pouring, or perhaps once they took it out boiling outside the pot and it became nevelah and after returning it was not permitted and forbids the rest until sixty against it. From this it appears in a fish where a sheretz was found or a piece of sheretz in its head and cooked thus with other fish one needs sixty against the whole head of the fish, for one law and one reason with what is found in its innards; and further proof from Orach Chaim general rule 36 regarding an apple with a worm — attached issur requires sixty in cooking against the whole apple if the apple itself does not have sixty against the worm; and so I saw in Maharil, etc. — peel the place for its taste is nullified in sixty even in cooking if there is sixty in the piece where it was found; and so he ruled on a piece of carp found in the head of a cooked fish to nullify its taste if there is sixty in the head of the fish against it. However per the reasoning it does not seem attached, for it rests where gravy can come from all sides; and since chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim is only rabbinic as I wrote, one may lean to be lenient here too and it is not chatichah na'asah nevelah — see siman 72 seif 3 what Taz and Shach wrote there.`,
    '4#ה': `Outside the gravy and issur fell on it. Meaning while still boiling the issur fell; but if first cold milk fell and afterward fell into a meat dish — it is obvious the meat itself with the rest of the dish nullifies the milk, since it was not forbidden until it came to the dish, and then the prohibition begins and nullification also begins in everything in the pot — so Orach Chaim general rule 28; and he wrote further that even if he raises that piece alone outside while boiling it is not forbidden and is not like attached issur, etc.`,
    '4#ו': `But if part of the piece is in the gravy, etc. This is R' Yehuda's view that we mentioned in seif 1 regarding a drop of milk on a meat piece — only Rama ruled here like his words in other issurim; and I already wrote this contradicts what Rama himself wrote in seif 2; and we hold like Rashi that even if part is in the gravy it is as wholly outside the gravy, stringently; nevertheless here in other issurim it is reasonable to rely on Rama that it does not become nevelah, per R' Yehuda's view.`,
    '4#ז': `If wet issur mixed with wet heter, etc. The reason in Beit Yosef in name of Mordechai: specifically in the piece itself from importance, for the issur pieces are recognizable and distinct — chatichah na'asah nevelah; but in wet with wet such as wine in wine or forbidden water in water, the issur is not recognizable and distinct and there is no issur importance — the second heter mixes with the first — end of his words; and Maharshal wrote ch. 3 siman 60: nevertheless it appears to me to distinguish that this is specifically when mixed not in the manner of cooking; but if it is the manner of cooking through imparting taste — issur mixed there and afterward fell from the gravy into permitted food — one needs sixty against all the gravy that fell, not to consider how much of the issur was absorbed in proportion in this gravy; but wet with wet such as wine in wine or water in forbidden water we can say we do not say all the permitted became nevelah, especially since it is rabbinic, and if more permitted was added afterward it is permitted — so it appears from his words that even not in case of loss we do not say in wet that became nevelah contrary to Rama here.`,
    '4#ח': `And if dry mixed with dry. Meaning not in the manner of cooking together — then its law is the issur is nullified in majority; therefore if first mixed not in majority one need not afterward majority against the whole first mixture; but if they cooked together certainly we say chatichah na'asah nevelah and need sixty in permitted against the whole piece. Tur wrote for R' Tam's view that even in other issurim "possible to squeeze it out" forbids and chatichah na'asah nevelah specifically in noten ta'am when there was not sixty in permitted to nullify the issur absorbed in it, but if forbidden in mashahu, even if forbidden due to its species forbidden in mashahu for R' Yehuda, the whole does not become nevelah — end of his words; and the source of this law is from what is stated in ch. Gid HaNasheh (daf 100) that Rav expounded: once taste entered a piece, chatichah na'asah nevelah and forbids all the pieces because they are its species; and Abaye establishes it there that a nevelah piece was first cooked with one permitted piece and received taste from the nevelah, and afterward the nevelah was removed and many permitted pieces remained with the piece that was first forbidden — we say the piece that was forbidden became chatichah na'asah nevelah and forbids all the permitted pieces even if there is sixty against it, for we hold like R' Yehuda that min b'mino in mashahu; and Tosafot challenged: why do I need that the forbidden piece was forbidden from the nevelah giving taste in it — behold even in mashahu it can forbid it because it is its species, as it forbids another afterward in mashahu; and R' Tam answered: granted it itself becomes forbidden in any amount, nevertheless we do not say it becomes nevelah to forbid all the others, since it itself was only forbidden in any amount — end of his words; explained in this that although for R' Yehuda there is min b'mino in mashahu, nevertheless this is specifically when it was first forbidden in mashahu; but if afterward it cooked in its species it has no power to forbid if there is sixty against the piece that was forbidden, and we do not forbid from min b'mino in mashahu since the substance of issur is not here, only that it absorbed from issur in mashahu — therefore the other permitted pieces were permitted; but if the piece was first forbidden from much issur that gave taste in it, then it forbids in mashahu its species and sixty does not help against it. And since we say this reasoning in mashahu in min b'mino for R' Yehuda from Torah, as stated at the beginning of ch. Kisuy HaDam from the verse of blood of the bull — all the more we say thus in rabbinic mashahu such as chametz on Pesach and yayin nesech; certainly whatever was first forbidden due to its species in mashahu does not forbid afterward if it fell to others and there is sixty against it, for then mashahu does not forbid; and therefore Tur wrote even if forbidden due to its species forbidden in mashahu for R' Yehuda — meaning from Torah as I explained; and all the more chametz on Pesach for us whether in its species or not at sixty, for if forbidden in mashahu it does not become nevelah if it would forbid afterward in mashahu — we do not say two mashahu. And so I saw in Perishah that brought in name of Maharshal to explain the language "even" that Tur wrote per the intent I wrote; only he challenged on this that since its prohibition is in mashahu, what difference whether in the first mixture or the second — granted in mashahu of R' Yehuda it only forbids in its species, and when it afterward fell in heter that is not its species we estimate as if the issur itself fell there and nullify it at sixty, because the issur itself does not forbid there except at sixty, for specifically in its species it forbids in mashahu; not so in chametz on Pesach that even if we estimate as if the issur alone fell to the second mixtures, even in not its species nevertheless it forbids in mashahu — and if so what does the second mixture help more than the first — end of his words; honor to him in its place, but he did not analyze Tosafot properly, for there it deals that the forbidden piece fell in its species as we brought above, and nevertheless it does not forbid in mashahu — we do not say two mashahu. And all poskim already brought R' Tam's view above, and so Orach Chaim general rule 28 in name of Sefer HaTerumah, and Maharshal ch. Gid HaNasheh siman 58, and Rama in Levush on chametz on Pesach and yayin nesech forbidden in mashahu did not become nevelah, and if it returned and fell into another pot it is nullified in majority if not recognized, and if recognized throw it away and the rest are permitted — end of his words. And per this we must explain what Shulchan Aruch ruled in Orach Chaim siman 447 seif 1 that the law of chametz mixture on Pesach in mashahu is like other issurim at sixty — in the first mixture; but in the second mixture it does not forbid in mashahu others, since the first mixture was only through mashahu. And Maharach explained the "even" that Tur wrote above also for chametz on Pesach — that we do not say it became nevelah, only that Tur wrote this regarding chametz mixture after Pesach; on this Tur speaks — not that we need not say it became nevelah implies that during Pesach it is forbidden; and this is not the intent, for from Tur's ruling that even for R' Yehuda in its species in mashahu it did not become nevelah, all the more in chametz even during Pesach since its mashahu is only rabbinic; and so appears primary; only we must explain that which Tosafot wrote above in name of R' Tam that we do not say two mashahu — there it deals that the issur piece was recognizable and they did not permit there except its discharge that does not forbid in mashahu; and therefore in chametz on Pesach too one may not be lenient except when the piece was forbidden in mashahu and returned and fell to another and they removed it — its discharge does not forbid in mashahu as long as there is sixty against it; but in wet with wet mixed in this way one may not be lenient, since the first wet was already forbidden in its mixture — the second mixture does not help afterward, for that issur is never nullified; and likewise a piece in dry forbidden in mashahu and not recognized remains in issur, and if we say it is nullified in the majority of others this depends on the dispute Shulchan Aruch brought in siman 447 seif 9, and one should be stringent; and so Levush ruled there to be stringent in dry with dry; and therefore what he ruled here that if one does not recognize the forbidden piece on Pesach it is nullified in majority is not correct at all and also contradicts his own words — rather the issur remains forever in its issur on Pesach and we have no permission except if we recognize it and remove it, for its discharge does not forbid in mashahu since it was only forbidden first in mashahu — so it appears to me clear and correct.`,
    '5#א': `That steams inward, etc. Orach Chaim general rule 31 in name of Semak wrote this either way: if it steams into the gravy behold the drop is nullified at sixty; and if it does not steam into it, why should it forbid — nevertheless pour the food immediately on the other side not opposite the falling of the issur, and do not leave it to cool; thus after the drop fell opposite the gravy lest it steam more — end of his words; explained from this that the pot is forbidden and we do not consider it as if it fell into the food, only regarding permission of the food alone; and Maharshal wrote ch. Gid HaNasheh siman 37 on this: even the pot is permitted from law to cook meat again, only l'chatchila forbidden to use it even for meat that appears as if forbidden in milk — end of his words; and so appears primary, as I will mention below.`,
    '5#ב': `And it steams. Meaning perhaps it will steam and there is concern for this.`,
    '5#ג': `Until near the gravy, etc. Per Beit Yosef's explanation it should be that the more it steams the more permission — for there is much moisture in that place of the drop's falling and its spread — requires much study why he said so much that there is not sixty against the drop, rather he should have said perhaps it only steams a little and there is not sixty in that place; and further such language implies he wants to enlarge the measure and afterward said there is not sixty there, implying he comes to diminish it. And one must say that all views Tur brings do not hold that moisture inside the vessel can nullify the drop, for who knows how much moisture is there, as we say in siman 98 that we estimate in the whole pot for we do not know how much discharges from it — so too here; and although Beit Yosef brings in name of Maharam in Mordechai: since it does not spread in all its walls so that one could say all moisture in the walls helps nullify — for ease of expression he wrote thus that even if we knew the measure of moisture there to nullify, nevertheless it would not help, for moisture does not steam at all; and therefore in this plain explanation the more it spreads the more it forbids that place and is not nullified at all; and the continuation of Tur's words is thus: at first he wrote there is issur in the drop in all its surroundings, and afterward explains how far the issur reaches — Maharam wrote if there is sixty times sixty it helps, from which two stringencies and one leniency follow: one stringency that even if it fell opposite the food we do not say the food nullifies it; a second stringency that the pot remains forbidden, for this matter is like a nevelah piece that fell into a pot and was nullified and you recognize it — certainly it remains in its issur; so too in this place where issur spread it becomes actual nevelah forever. And one leniency that one may pour the gravy immediately from the pot while hot since there is sixty in the food against the whole issur. And Sefer HaMitzvot disputes and holds the reverse: namely he holds there is doubt whether the drop steams, or we say it does not move from its place at all — not as Beit Yosef wrote that there is doubt how far steaming reaches, whether in all or in part, for the doubt in the Gemara ch. Dam Chatat is not in this way but if steaming goes from the chatat dish outside its place in the rest of the vessel and forbids it — so too here; and therefore he wrote here if it fell opposite the food it is permitted either way: if it steams in all of it — meaning the drop steams from its place to another place, and he used language "in the whole vessel" following Talmudic language — but in truth he did not intend here except if it has steaming at all outside its place, behold it is nullified through the food, for since it has steaming it enters inward into the food and the food nullifies it; and if it does not steam at all but stands in its place, there is no issur at all since it does not spread from its place — it does not enter inward; and therefore Tur did not need to bring this distinction, for it is simple; and Beit Yosef's words are not difficult, and one enters to resolve as explained in his words; and behold per this the pot is also permitted: if it steams inward it is as if it fell into the food itself, and then the pot is also permitted, for we consider the pot wall as part of the food itself; and if it does not steam, the food cooked in it afterward should not be forbidden — why is the first different; and afterward he said if it fell opposite the empty place, a large part of the pot is forbidden, lest it steam a large part, in any case not until the gravy, for otherwise it would be like falling opposite the food that we mentioned, since here too there is connection of the drop with the food; and you might still say there is permission for food that has sixty against that part of the pot that was forbidden — therefore he said perhaps a very large part spread so there is not sixty in the food against that part — therefore forbidden to pour through there, for in this part of the pot the issur remains forever and nullification does not help; and one may still challenge why he needed to say until near the gravy — this closeness is unnecessary, he should have said it steams outside the gravy, for the main intent is that it not connect to the gravy; one may say also because he wants to rule there is no remedy except to leave it until it cools, and no permission to pour from the other side where issur did not spread, or pierce from the bottom of the vessel as other poskim mention — therefore he said there is concern lest steaming stopped near the gravy and if the vessel moves from its place it will reach the forbidden place — therefore leave until it cools specifically; and what he said so much that there is not sixty against the drop — he does not speak of the drop alone but with its spread — and this is clear.`,
    '6#א': `People practice to forbid, etc. Meaning even leaving until it cools does not help; and so it appears from Orach Chaim general rule 31 that wrote: people practice to forbid the food even if there is sixty in the food against the drop, since it fell opposite the empty place — then certainly it only steams on the pot walls alone, and we fear perhaps afterward once the pot boiled it touched that part of the wall that was forbidden, or perhaps it touches now at the time of pouring before it cools — end of his words; and in Perishah it implies nevertheless there is permission even per the custom to leave until it cools, and he only was stringent to pour before cooling — and the matters are clear as I wrote, and so Terumat HaChacham general rule 55.`,
    '6#ב': `But if it fell opposite the fire. Meaning while it still stands by the fire; but after they removed it, although still boiling, nevertheless it does not burn and dry; and although this is simple, I saw one who erred in this.`,
    '7#א': `Such as erev Shabbat, etc. Terumat HaChacham general rule 55 wrote the same for any great need such as for guests, great loss, or a poor person per the teacher's judgment — end of his words; and it is written in responsum Mahari Minz siman 15 that the custom is to tell them the reason why he is lenient so they are not astonished why sometimes he forbids and sometimes permits.`,
    '7#ב': `Even not opposite the fire. Maharshal wrote ch. Gid HaNasheh siman 37: nevertheless the pot is forbidden, lest it did not steam inward and the place of the drop is forbidden — therefore pour it from another side and one need not wait until cooling since it is erev Shabbat.`,
    '7#ג': `Through sixty. Meaning sixty in the food against the drop alone; Orach Chaim general rule 31 wrote: if a drop of milk fell on the pot with water by the fire and within me'eit l'eit they cooked meat in it, even if there was not sixty in the water against the drop the meat is permitted, for if it did not pass first into the pot, also the second time it will not pass, and the meat is permitted b'dieved; and even if it passes inward first, there is only milk in the pot according to the estimated amount in the water, for in permitted we do not consider the whole piece as absorption itself and against that small matter presumably there is sixty in the meat.`,
    '7#ד': `And if milk spilled, etc. So Hagahot Sh"D in name of Maharam; and Orach Chaim general rule 31 wrote in this in name of R' Yitzchak of Corbeil that sixty is required from what we estimate of milk in appearance under the pot — end of his words; and Maharshal ch. 25 siman 38 wrote this ruling did not appear correct to him, for it is only kli sheini; and he wrote one may distinguish where the pot stands by the fire, that the fire's heat in the pot boils the lower from cooling and absorbs well; and so appears primary — end of his words; and per my humble opinion without boiling of the pot there is no difficulty, for since it was heated by the fire and is hot where yad soledes bo applies, the milk in it is like kli rishon; and Maharam who permits deals when it spilled in a place of heating that is not hot from the fire and yad soledes bo does not apply there; and so appears primary for law.`,
    '7#ה': `It is also like kli sheini. Per my humble opinion this too is specifically when that place is not boiling and yad soledes bo does not apply, for otherwise it is like kli rishon, as I wrote below.`,
    '7#ו': `And if the stream did not break, etc. Terumat HaDeshen brought in Beit Yosef who wrote: if yad soledes bo in the milk where it touched the meat, it is like iruy from kli rishon and the meat is forbidden; and Beit Yosef wrote on this: and it appears the meat was cold, for otherwise why need he say the milk is considered iruy — behold even completely cold milk forbids the meat, for it is cold into hot; and since so, that which he said the meat is forbidden is only kedei kelipah, for hot into cold only forbids kedei kelipah — end of his words; Beit Yosef holds what touched the milk at the pot is the pot below and the milk above — therefore he is stringent with hot meat and cold milk to forbid all of it, and lenient with cold meat and hot milk that it only forbids kedei kelipah; and after this explanation Rama followed here; and per my humble opinion it is astonishing, for whatever touches the pot there is neither upper nor lower, but they have the law of touching each other, and its law is explained in siman 105 seif 3 that if one is cold and the other hot it only forbids kedei kelipah; and so I found in Maharshal ch. Gid HaNasheh siman 38 who compares this to touching — only he is stringent in touching as will be explained in siman 105; and therefore he challenged Terumat HaDeshen that I mentioned: why need the milk be boiling — behold since the meat is boiling it is forbidden as the law of cold and hot touching per his view; and he resolved that the milk flowed under the meat pot — behold the milk is lower and does not forbid if it were cold; per our view there is leniency if we say it is like touching each other, except one may not be lenient in what Beit Yosef and Rama were stringent; nevertheless per my humble opinion, since Rama wrote in siman 105 seif 10 that even in salting one should forbid where there is no loss in touching each other, all the more here with heat by fire one must be stringent here too, even with cold meat and hot milk where there is no great loss — as it appears to me.`,
    '7#ז': `And that the pot began to boil, etc. Terumat HaChacham general rule 55 concluded on this: and it appears to me if it did not begin to boil and fell on the cover, what is in the pot is not forbidden at all even without sixty, for it is still like cold and does not steam into what is in the pot — end of his words; and it appears regarding the cover itself it is considered boiling since it rests on the pot by the fire — and so one may explain Terumat HaChacham's language. And regarding the main law Rama wrote here, its law is like falling on the pot wall — Maharshal ch. Kol HaBasar siman 46 disputes and wrote: although boiling rises to the cover, nevertheless the gravy does not join with it — if there is not sixty on the cover against the issur that fell on it the cover becomes nevelah — end of his words; and so it appears to my humble opinion, for the pot opposite the gravy differs because the gravy itself is always there, not so the cover — perhaps at the time the drop fell boiling did not reach the cover itself; and a Torah doubt is stringent — only in rabbinic issur one may be lenient — as it appears to me.`,
    '8#_': `Like two pots, etc. From this reason it appears to me to permit if they baked in a milk panada oven and afterward in that place while boiling they placed a pot with meat inside, for as long as there is no milk in appearance in that place in the oven it is like a milk pot and two pots do not forbid each other.`,
    '9#_': `But milk of yours, etc. Meaning from its mouth in the place where the wick burns — that side is considered through the fire itself; so Hagahot Sh"D in name of Maharai — automatically if it dripped from another side of the vessel where the wick is inside, it is not called boiling to forbid what drips on it; and greater than this he wrote there in the hagahah in name of Maharam: if a hot drop of milk fell on a knife or other cold vessel, one needs only scraping where the drop fell, since the drop ceased at the time of falling and did not come in a splash; and specifically when it falls on wood, but if it fell on iron stab ten times in the ground and enough — end of his words; and he distinguishes there between this law of Maharam, which is hot from boiling by fire, and the law of "milk of yours" above, which is from the fire itself — so is the explanation of this matter. Written in Maharil Laws of Pesach: one who purifies by iruy who pours boiling water on the vessel to purify it should beware to pour the boiling water specifically from the vessel in which it was cooked, which is called kli rishon, and not draw from the vessel containing the boiling water with another vessel and pour from it to purify, because the drawing vessel with it is kli sheini and the boiling water was nullified in cooking before he purifies with it; however if he leaves the drawing vessel within the vessel in which they cook the boiling water, and while it is there raises boiling water — it is properly called also kli rishon — end of his words. From these words it implies that if one draws from kli rishon and removes boiling water, it is afterward called kli sheini and has the law of iruy; and I wondered greatly at this, for behold while still in kli rishon the vessel one inserts in it is also kli rishon, for we purify vessels in kli rishon and discharge from them what is absorbed; and so we hold regarding inserting a forbidden spoon into a pot that forbids the spoon even if he removes the spoon from it — and how can one say here that when he removes the drawing vessel from kli rishon it becomes kli sheini because kli rishon name came upon it; who gives it another name afterward — behold kli rishon is its name even if removed from the fire, so too what was removed from boiling water inside kli rishon. And further, if he waited there a bit, Maharil concedes the name kli rishon remains — and if so who measures how long the waiting should be, as Tur wrote in Orach Chaim siman 452: Rif wrote one leaves vessels in the yorah until they discharge — and Rabbenu Avraham Azriel wrote who can measure, etc. — so too we may say here who can measure the waiting in this. And further proof from ch. Kirah: R' Shmuel bar Avdimi said: I entered after Rabbi to the bathhouse on Shabbat, etc., and he told him take with a kli sheini so the heat lessens; and do not err and say he means take with kli sheini from the bath which is kli rishon and we learn the drawing vessel is kli sheini — this is not so, for then he should have said simply take a vessel and give; and further it is clarified that what he said kli sheini refers to take — meaning at the time of taking it should be kli sheini; and certainly one cannot say thus, for at the time of taking from boiling water it is kli rishon since it is inside it; and also he told him it should be kli sheini after it leaves the water, for he said take kli sheini — learn he spoke at the time of taking and not about removing afterward; and further when he removed it, who put kli sheini name on it — can every kli rishon after it cools become kli sheini? And further Tosafot wrote there the main distinction between kli rishon and kli sheini is that kli sheini cools because its walls are not hot — therefore it goes on cooling; and this certainly has walls boiling, for they were heated in kli rishon's boiling water — only a simple matter: Rabbi said take from the water with some object and put it in a kli sheini — meaning pour it into a kli sheini like every kli sheini in the Talmud; and know that if there were novelty here that kli rishon becomes kli sheini, he would have stated this novelty where we learn from it that kli sheini does not cook well; also Tur in Orach Chaim siman 318 when he brought this law of take kli sheini, etc., wrote only if it was hot in kli sheini one may put into it, etc., and did not write that one draws from kli rishon and nevertheless puts into it — rather plainly this is not so; and explicit proof further from what Agudah brought in Orach Chaim general rule 60: a fly found in a bowl is permitted, for it is a double doubt: perhaps it fell in kli sheini, and even if you say kli rishon, perhaps when they removed the fly — namely in the spoon — there was sixty in the spoon — end of his words; and likewise regarding removing a forbidden egg in a spoon, and so Shach and Tur regarding removing a fly in a spoon siman 107 — behold they required sixty in the gravy in the spoon against the fly, meaning the spoon is considered kli rishon even after removing from the pot, for all the time it was in the pot there is no issur from the fly because there was sixty there, only after removing it in the spoon it forbids; and on this they said perhaps there was sixty in the spoon — learn the spoon still has kli rishon name even after removing; and so proven from Rama's ruling in siman 107: but if a little of the food was with the issur in the spoon and returned to the pot, sixty is needed also against the little food, for it became nevelah in the spoon — learn the spoon is kli rishon in what it draws from the pot and forbids the gravy in it until sixty. And I again saw in Terumat HaDeshen siman 185 regarding absorption inside the spoon becoming nevelah: one may say after removing the spoon from the boiling gravy, all absorption inside it is considered like kli sheini and neither discharged nor absorbed — all the more per the view that absorbed issur does not forbid absorbed heter; it appears to reconcile this with what I wrote: there he removes an empty spoon from the pot and there is only what is absorbed in it — in this we certainly say there is not so much heat in an empty vessel to discharge and absorb in what is absorbed in the absorption; and even in actual kli rishon removed from fire and emptied it appears to say thus — not so when there is still boiling gravy or food inside, certainly the spoon has kli rishon name like the pot; otherwise certainly there is no reason Terumat HaDeshen would dispute Shach, Tur, and Agudah I cited — rather certainly as I wrote. And I again saw in Tosafot ch. Am MiMazikin daf 33 s.v. kinisa: R' Yehuda would say it is a mitzvah min hamuvchar to purify each board alone and throw on it from that vessel itself that is boiling on the fire and that suffices, although boiling ceased when moving it from the fire; and some place a bowl inside a pan on the fire until boiling rises in the bowl and from that bowl they purify — and in this R' Yehuda is uncertain whether this helps as kli rishon or not, and left the matter to be stringent and forbade purifying with them, considering them kli sheini; and for melichah of chicken too it is forbidden, considering it kli rishon — end of his words. Behold Tosafot were uncertain here and raised to stringency; except Rosh there wrote in name of R' Yehuda that it is like kli rishon since he removed it from the yorah boiling; and so Tur in Yoreh De'ah siman 131 in name of R' Yehuda; and one must say they had a different text in Tosafot; and from Rama's words per Hagahot Maimoniyot in Orach Chaim siman 451 it implies he ruled stringently that it is not kli rishon, for he wrote there: and pour on them boiling water from kli rishon, etc. — therefore it appears to be stringent like Tosafot's words.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#א': `That piece is forbidden, etc. One must understand: since it deals when he preceded and removed, absorption cannot discharge — therefore that piece too should be permitted, for what fell on it the meat did not receive milk taste, for kafeilah tasted and said there is no milk taste, and one need not say it absorbed and discharged; nevertheless it became nevelah, for it deals when he preceded and removed that there is no concern of discharge at all. And one must say Rambam inferred this from Rav's matter who said a kezayit of meat fell into a cauldron of meat — forbidden; and we establish it when he preceded and removed. And it is hard how he ruled and said forbidden — it must be noten ta'am, for if a gentile tastes and there is no taste it is permitted; therefore that which in the boiling cauldron what is absorbed is not discharged means completely that taste was discharged, but certainly some taste discharges until weak taste remains that kafeilah taste does not sense; and also what discharged is not full taste only weak taste, for for Rav his boiling cannot discharge full taste — therefore the milk is entirely permitted for it is not discharged; and weak taste does not forbid its mixture; and we require full taste to forbid mixture, not weak taste; but the meat since at first it absorbed full taste, although it returned and discharged and the taste became weak — behold it was already forbidden; or one may say, and more correct, there is doubt perhaps it discharged weak taste, and since chatichah na'asah nevelah even weak taste forbids as it is forbidden and one cannot rely on tasting — therefore that piece is forbidden, perhaps weak taste remained and cannot be tasted; but the cauldron is permitted since he preceded and removed; and if it discharged weak taste — what of it, it is not called min b'mino since its taste is weak from milk taste in the cauldron — then it is exceptional the second time, min in not its species, and is nullified; but Rambam for us, when he preceded and removed and does not require sixty — one cannot say it discharged weak taste, for all is rabbinic min b'mino and we are lenient in doubt — requires study.`,
    '1#ב': `But if he did not manage [in time], etc. That which suffices with sixty — behold it is a kezayit of nevelah milk; and if so it should require sixty-one zayitim — this is not difficult, for forbidden items nullify each other; and if so regarding meat taste the forbidden milk also joins, for in the end its taste divides into sixty parts and there is no taste; and regarding the forbidden milk a kezayit of meat also joins as I wrote, for in any case its taste divides into sixty parts — and do not challenge; and Peri Chadash did not stand on this and was challenged; and what one may infer that it discharges and pierces and discharges until the whole pot is forbidden — see below siman 94, if God wills. And one may infer for Rambam's views: that which we taught a pot in which he cooked meat and cooked milk in it through noten ta'am — the Gemara explains through kafeilah — what does it help, perhaps it absorbed and became nevelah and returned and discharged and needs sixty; and see below whether we say absorbed becomes chatichah na'asah nevelah; and for the view that we say thus it is difficult; and one must say poskim already divided that issur absorbed does not go from piece to piece without gravy, but absorbed in a vessel discharges — the reason is that absorption clings more in food and is hard to separate from absorption in a vessel that has nothing to cling to; and per this what we say meat that absorbed milk becomes chatichah na'asah nevelah and the abundance of milk in the pot does not join with it to nullify the meat is because it is inside a separate piece — so Ran too, and what is outside is not connected at all — this is in absorption inside food; but absorbed in a vessel certainly does not cling to the vessel and is still connected with the food around it; and afterward although the pot absorbs from food there is no interruption and separation, and everything inside the pot is one connection without interruption — noten ta'am does not apply at all — requires study. And from the matter of curdling in kosher hide that is basar b'chalav — we taught regarding noten ta'am, and Rambam explained gentile tasting — it is not difficult also, since it was pickled in it from above it is like cooked and absorbed and returns and discharges — what does tasting help; and see Mordechai end of Kol HaBasar who is concerned for this; and one must say Rambam holds in basar b'chalav something forbidden through pickling that is rabbinic we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, as many authors hold. And one may infer Tosafot in ch. Gid HaNasheh daf 99 side b s.v. except in what, etc. — they toiled to resolve Rashi's difficulty in the Gemara: lentils fell into lentils, etc., that challenges "except in what — is it not at sixty," and Rashi challenged perhaps it imparts taste to it and there is no noten ta'am; and see Tosafot who strained themselves to resolve this difficulty — and what is the difficulty for them, behold the lentils absorbed the lentils and became nevelah, and when it discharged it cannot be tasted — for the view that at one hundred requires at least one hundred; and what is Rashi's difficulty for Tosafot, and what is this strain that they strained themselves there for Rashi — it appears they hold like R' Eliezer that we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in all issurim except basar b'chalav; but for Tosafot it is difficult and strained to say they also said thus for R' Eliezer's views; and if this were so, Rashi's words would be justified, and also Rosh strained himself to resolve Rashi's difficulty as explained there — and if they held chatichah na'asah nevelah in all issurim there is no place for Rashi's difficulty; but nevertheless it is strained; and it appears more that Tosafot still have difficulty whence to challenge that perhaps this tanna holds like the first tanna below — namely the Sages of R' Yehuda that "possible to squeeze out" is permitted, and even chatichah na'asah nevelah not — especially per Yerushalmi this mishnah of lentils that fell into lentils is R' Meir who holds noten ta'am li'fegam is forbidden, and the Sages of R' Yehuda is on the majority R' Meir; and therefore Tosafot needed to strain in the answer.`,
    '1#ג': `Until it can discharge. One may infer: nevertheless there is no Torah prohibition in basar b'chalav here, for it is explained in the Gemara in which cooking they spoke — in cooking that others eat it, namely like food of the court; and this is that he removed immediately — there is no food of the court here, and it is only rabbinic prohibition, and we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah; and it is difficult for those great ones who hold we do not say — why does the Gemara strain to change Rav's matter that challenges "why is milk permitted — nevelah milk," that he preceded and removed very quickly, and we do not change that it did not wait so long as cooking of the court — if so the absorbed milk was not Torah-forbidden and is not chatichah na'asah nevelah; and the great pious Rav R' Yonah of Prague, may his honor rest, agreed. As Rosh wrote in Shabbat: this beef is not cooked except by fire, nevertheless its moisture cooks immediately — only the braita for lashes was changed and there is no measure in moisture, and beef requires cooking of the court; but regarding karet it is forbidden from the Torah — he spoke well, lips worth kissing; but I wonder why Rambam did not mention this law in his work regarding lashes at least. I returned home and saw in Tosefta Chullin ch. 25 that teaches: as one is liable for eating it, how much must he eat to be liable — enough that if eaten it would be eaten from cooking — since it asked how much to eat and not how much to cook to be liable, the main matter depends only on cooking — it immediately obligates; only for eating, if they ate before it was cooked like food of the court and ate it raw, behold it is not in the manner of enjoyment; and this tanna holds the prohibition of eating basar b'chalav is from "you shall not eat them raw," etc., or "you shall not eat" regarding disqualified offerings, as Rabbi's view in the Gemara daf 115 — if so that emerging from the prohibition of eating, one is only liable in the manner of enjoyment, and therefore exempt if eaten uncooked; but per our holding that we derive from "you shall not cook," and since it derived in language of cooking, one is liable even not in the manner of enjoyment — this case fell in a pit; and therefore Rambam omitted it — requires study, for it is hard to say a dispute regarding not in the manner of enjoyment when we do not find it explicitly in the Gemara.`,
    '2#א': `And if he stirred from beginning to end. The Mechaber ruled like Rambam that one must stir from beginning to end, and Rama wrote in the gloss: and some say stirring at the beginning alone suffices, and so is primary; and it is hard, for Tur wrote on Rambam's words that it appears one need only stir or cover immediately at the time of falling, and even if he ceased afterward — and Mahari ibn Chabib wrote that Rambam's view is also thus; and what he wrote stirring from beginning to end was because at the outset he had to mention stirring at the end — for otherwise why would everything in the pot be forbidden — and therefore he also mentioned stirring at the end in the conclusion; and per this the Mechaber should not have been silent and should have explained that stirring at the beginning alone suffices — but he followed his view in Beit Yosef that Rambam requires stirring at beginning and end, and the Rav in the gloss that stirring at the beginning alone suffices; and this is what he wrote: and likewise if he stirred at the beginning, etc.; and he did not write this law in language of "some say" because the Mechaber's words can also be explained thus, as Mahari ibn Chabib explained Rambam's words; but the Mechaber's own view is not so; nevertheless the view of Mahari ibn Chabib and the Rav is primary, and so from Rashi (daf 109a) — see there; and so Maharshal ch. 25 siman 45 and Acharonim; and it appears Tur did not intend to challenge but to explain Rambam's words.`,
    '2#ב': `And if he did not stir at all. The Mechaber wrote: if there is sixty in the pot against the drop of milk that fell, it forbids only the piece alone. One might say when there is not sixty in the pot it should also be permitted since he did not stir at all and it only imparted taste in that piece itself — as in the Gemara and poskim, and explicitly in Raavad's challenges ch. 9 of Ma'achalot Asurot — see there; and Darkei Moshe brings it briefly; and it appears what requires sixty in the pot is when part is in the gravy, for the Rav is in doubt whether we hold like R' Yehuda who mixes taste in all or like Rashi — therefore the pot is permitted either way and the piece is forbidden lest he does not mix taste in all and the pot does not join.`,
    '2#ג': `And likewise if it fell into the pot. This is Rambam's language, and Tur wrote: I do not understand his words — if the pieces are all in the gravy why need to stir, and if not in the gravy what does stirring help if he stirs afterward — end of his words; and the Rav in the gloss: and some dispute, etc.; and although Beit Yosef brought in name of Mahari ibn Chabib that Rambam also holds thus — nevertheless the Rav wrote in language of "some dispute" since the plain meaning does not imply this; and this is primary in Rambam's words as explained: since it is not known on which piece it fell, the whole pot enters doubt and joins to nullify — similar to two pots in siman 111.`,
    '2#ד': `And if he recognizes it he throws it away. When there is sixty against the piece; and if he does not recognize it the gravy is permitted when there is sixty in the pieces and gravy, and all the pieces are forbidden if the issur piece is fit to be honored — for we hold below siman 101 that in basar b'chalav it is issur from the substance itself; and if fit to be honored it is not nullified in a thousand; and if not fit to be honored it is nullified in majority as below siman 109 — see there.`,
    '4#א': `And then the piece itself returns, etc. The Acharonim challenged that below siman 106 he ruled that nevertheless the piece is forbidden — and perhaps it deals below in not its species, which is a Torah issur concern per the stringent view; but here it deals with milk in meat, which is min b'mino, as Rashba wrote in his responsum that it is the manner of cooking — and if so it is nullified in majority by Torah law and here there is only rabbinic issur, and he ruled like the lenient view. Also certainly per the view that "possible to squeeze out" forbids or permits — whether the issur fully discharged or something remained — see Tosafot in his responsum. But now it fell into another pot, etc., for they wrote it is certain that it all discharged and one does not forbid from absorption; and if so, since poskim disagree whether "possible to squeeze out" forbids or permits, in this that if it fell into a pot to forbid the pot — in this we are lenient as I wrote — it is certainly doubt and does not forbid; and therefore Shulchan Aruch intended when he said and then the piece itself is permitted — meaning not to forbid the pot, but regarding the piece itself certainly the issur remains since issur is upon it — and this is his intent in siman 106 — see there and investigate.`,
    '4#ב': `Some say we state chatichah na'asah nevelah in all issurim, etc. And Peri Chadash ruled thus from Ran's view in Yerushalmi who taught in Orlah: lentils fell into lentils — how much must he add? Rabbi says against all of them, and R' Yosi says against what fell — and this is because chatichah na'asah nevelah, for Rabbi holds against all of them, from which we learn Rabbi holds chatichah na'asah nevelah in all issurim. And this challenge is also for Raavad in his hasagah who holds we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah except for the view that min b'mino is not nullified — that view is a refutation, for Rabbi holds it is nullified and nevertheless chatichah na'asah nevelah. But it appears for practical law the opposite — the leniency of R' Eliezer from this: for Rabbi holds in Chullin min b'mino is not nullified; if chatichah na'asah nevelah, behold it is min b'mino — and it works out for the view that a thing forbidden due to another thing is called by the name of that species, and therefore lentils forbidden from the lentils are called by the name of the lentils and are species in not its species. But for Rabbenu Tam and Rashbam who hold it is its species — it is difficult, and forced to say terumah lentils are rabbinic; and Tosafot explained above that fish broth is mere steam and is rabbinic, and in rabbinic issur R' Yosi holds min b'mino is nullified, as many sugyot in the Gemara, and one at the end of Beitzah implies that even in rabbinic issur min b'mino is not nullified. Therefore it appears they hold one in its companion does not depend, as Rashba's view — that even though there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah, nevertheless "possible to squeeze it out" forbids — and if so those lentils were forbidden from the start by the lentils and do not return to permission; and even though he adds lentils to nullify the taste of the lentils, nevertheless the bodies of the lentils are forbidden, and therefore Rabbi requires that he add to nullify them one in two, for the bodies of the lentils are forbidden for they absorbed the lentils and need nullification one in two, and min b'mino is not nullified does not apply, for since there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah even Rabbenu Tam concedes it is not its species and nullified; and R' Yosi holds "possible to squeeze it out" is permitted and the lentils were not forbidden and it suffices to nullify the taste of the lentils. And per this that which requires increase for the lentils is not for their taste, for their taste of terumah is the lentils, but it does not become nevelah except due to "possible to squeeze it out" forbids — it is species in not its species, and Rabbi's words are correct. And Shach wrote: that in other issurim chatichah na'asah nevelah is only rabbinic, for the Sages decreed because of basar b'chalav — and so Ran and Hagahot Maimoniyot, etc. And in truth this is not s.v., as Maharai"q wrote explicitly that many hold it is d'oraisa, and there is great practical difference whether d'oraisa or d'rabbanan in many matters.

And behold in Tosafot Chullin daf 108 side 2 s.v. amai they wrote explicitly, and these are their words: but in general chatichah na'asah nevelah is rabbinic and requires five against all of it, for from the Torah one does not need sixty except against the issur absorbed in it, etc. — end of his words. But the matter is difficult, for if so what is the challenge daf 100 s.v. keshekadim, etc., to Rabbenu Tam who says in all issurim chatichah na'asah nevelah — how did they hag'alah vessels from the law of large yorot; and likewise on daf 108 above s.v. shenafel, etc., they wrote in the law that the Torah only forbade beit yomo — they only hag'alah small vessels inside large ones that have sixty, etc. — and the matter is astonishing, for by Torah law there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah at all, and Tosafot contradict their words in one sugya, and forced to say Tosafot hold these were explicit permission in the Torah in hag'alah of the law, for otherwise how did they hag'alah large yorot — the Sages would not forbid what the Torah explicitly permitted — and this is extremely forced.

And one may say that certainly one may say it is d'oraisa and we derive by gezeirah shavah from basar b'chalav — only this depends on the dispute of Abaye and Rava: for Abaye who holds there it is its taste and not its substance, one may derive from basar b'chalav that it is not a novelty, for if so even without noten ta'am also, and if so it is not a novelty and we derive from it ta'am k'ikar — even this is included in the derivation, as there ta'am k'ikar makes it nevelah, so too in all issurim ta'am k'ikar and we make it nevelah. But for Rava who holds from basar b'chalav there is nothing to derive, for it is a novelty — only he derives ta'am k'ikar from elsewhere as Tosafot wrote; if so whence chatichah na'asah nevelah from basar b'chalav there is nothing to derive, and it is rabbinic — and if so that which Tosafot wrote rabbinic is correct per the conclusion that we hold like Rava. But what Tosafot challenged Rabbenu Tam from vessels of the law is for Abaye who also holds chatichah na'asah nevelah, which is what the Gemara answers in keshekadim and removed — and for his view we derive from basar b'chalav and it challenges well. And likewise what Tosafot wrote in s.v. shenafel — all is to settle Abaye's words, and even though Tosafot wrote Abaye retracted, nevertheless Abaye is challenged before his retraction from hag'alah of the law. And if this is correct in itself, nevertheless it is difficult for me that Tosafot's intent should revolve on this, and they should have mentioned — and one may say that certainly for R' Pinchas Kohen's view, as Tur ruled in siman 98, if a kezayit of issur fell in more than sixty, that although forbidden from the Torah, nevertheless if one ate from the mixture a kezayit he is not lashed, for it is not like the substance of issur — but in basar b'chalav if a kezayit of meat fell into sixty zayitim of milk and one ate a kezayit of milk he is lashed, for he ate from the substance of issur — and if so how can we derive chatichah na'asah nevelah from basar b'chalav, there he always eats the substance of issur — obviously chatichah na'asah nevelah; but here we see it is not similar to basar b'chalav, for in more than sixty he is not lashed — see Mordechai who wrote this reasoning explicitly, and learn that it is not the substance of issur and we do not compare to basar b'chalav at all and do not derive from it — and how shall we derive chatichah na'asah nevelah from it that it should be like the substance of issur? Here we see in this matter that we consider it like the substance of issur — they are not comparable. But for Rabbenu Tam who holds ta'am k'ikar — even one who eats a kezayit from sixty zayitim of the mixture is lashed — and if so it is fully comparable to basar b'chalav that it is the substance of issur, and we derive from it in every manner that there is chatichah na'asah nevelah — and if so that which Tosafot challenged in ch. 3 is for Rabbenu Tam as I wrote that it is fully comparable to basar b'chalav; and likewise in the passage beginning shenafel, etc., it is Rabbenu Tam's matter who spoke to the point and it is d'oraisa. But Tosafot themselves hold for halachah like R' Chaim Kohen, as the authors and Tur and Rosh wrote — and if so it is not d'oraisa and it is permitted.

And more it would appear that per the reasoning that a kid the Torah forbade and not milk — if so it is difficult for Rabbenu Tam how it can be that milk is not forbidden, behold the meat became forbidden and behold the milk absorbed from the fat and ta'am k'ikar is d'oraisa to obligate lashes per Rabbenu Tam's views — if so how can it be in reality that milk is not forbidden, and with what did the Torah limit milk from a kid — in the end it is forbidden and one is lashed for the lav of basar b'chalav — and one must say the Torah taught us that in other issurim besides basar b'chalav there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah — and if so there is great practical difference whether milk is forbidden due to itself — if so chatichah na'asah nevelah when two zayitim of milk cooked with a zayit of meat in a pot requires sixty against both zayitim; but if a kid is forbidden and not milk, only milk forbidden that received meat taste — one does not need sixty, only against the absorbed zayit. And per this reasoning in seif 4 it is proven that in other issurim there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah, and that which Rav himself expounded above regarding a piece of nevelah chatichah na'asah nevelah — one must say rabbinic — and these are Tosafot's words, all per the reasoning, but per the conclusion that we hold a kid and milk are forbidden by Torah and there is no limitation — well one may say all issurim of Torah that we derive by gezeirah shavah are permitted, and per the conclusion it does not apply.

But it is difficult for me in what the Gemara challenges: Rav holds a kid the Torah forbade and not milk, and Rav said half a zayit of meat and half a zayit of milk — one is lashed for eating it, and why, etc. And it is difficult for Rabbenu Tam, for milk absorbed issur of meat and ta'am k'ikar for Rabbenu Tam even for lashes — obviously he is lashed — and why is it needed, since it only absorbed from half a zayit of meat, even Rabbenu Tam concedes there are no lashes here, for never was the issur the full measure — for below we establish Rav's words who said one is lashed for eating it when it comes from a large yorah that at first a kezayit of meat and a kezayit of milk whole were cooked — and if so behold ta'am k'ikar and obviously he is lashed per Rabbenu Tam. And one must say in seif 4 reasoning that the Torah limited the milk — even ta'am k'ikar limited that meat taste should not forbid it, for otherwise it is not in reality that a kid is forbidden and not milk. And it challenges well, and if so my above reasoning does not apply — and perhaps this is Tosafot's intent: since the Torah limited that milk is not forbidden due to meat taste, learn the Torah's will is not to forbid meat by its taste at all — this is certainly against reason, that if milk fell in water it should forbid, and if it falls into milk it should not forbid — it is a degradation. And if so this chatichah na'asah nevelah in Torah issurim is clear, for from where shall we learn from meat cooked in milk? There meat does not forbid its mixture at all and there is no ta'am k'ikar d'oraisa in this. And one must say rabbinic.

But in truth it is difficult to reconcile this with Tosafot's words: what proof from other issurim that we say chatichah na'asah nevelah? Granted in other issurim one needs sixty against the absorbed issur d'oraisa as Tosafot wrote explicitly — they were stringent for chatichah na'asah nevelah since ta'am k'ikar is d'oraisa — not so in basar b'chalav per the reasoning that ta'am k'ikar is not d'oraisa and its taste does not forbid at all from the Torah — the Sages were stringent to make nevelah. And although from its place it is decided that Rav said explicitly in basar b'chalav — also one reason it is not comparable, for there meat is chatichah na'asah nevelah which is issur of substance, and here milk is forbidden only due to absorption. And further Tosafot should have brought it from its place where we see chatichah na'asah nevelah is rabbinic, not from other places that are not comparable at all — therefore one must say Tosafot follow their view that there is no lashes in ta'am k'ikar — and if so there is no challenge from a kid and not milk — and if so my above reasoning does not apply; and in any event it appears for R' Chaim Kohen in other issurim it is rabbinic, and for Rabbenu Tam d'oraisa — and so is correct.`,
    '4#ג': `But in basar b'chalav the Rav Shach challenged that it implies we are stringent in basar b'chalav beyond the law — and it is not so, for by law it is forbidden; if so there is no difficulty in the Gemara \\"why is milk permitted — it is nevelah milk,\\" and this difficulty is explained in Rashba and Ran. And I have difficulty: even Raavad and Mordechai, holders of this reasoning, hold that in basar b'chalav there is no difference between wet and dry — whence do we learn to innovate in other issurim a difference; and although to resolve Tosafot's difficulty from \\"a pitcher of water fell at the end\\" in tractate Avodah Zarah — nevertheless the matter itself is hard: what did the Sages see to divide equal things, and what distinguishes this from that. And in truth it is hard for me that they ruled explicitly against the view of Tosafot and Rabbenu Tam and Rashbam, who all hold in the matter of the piece that two sixties are required — for even gravy that is wet in wet is not nullified. And Tosafot challenged Rashi from this: since taste entered the piece, behold the gravy became chatichah na'asah nevelah and forbids all. And Tosafot challenged from this: a kid — the Torah forbade and not milk — nevertheless chatichah na'asah nevelah from the issur. And likewise the challenge from vessels from hag'alah also fits this reasoning — it appears like R' Eliezer: they hag'alah twice; so for Rabbenu Tam three times — for in the first time the water became forbidden and returned and hag'alah a second time and discharged the water, and then it did not become chatichah na'asah nevelah for it is wet in wet; and if so the vessel also did not absorb except proportionally, and the third hag'alah helps; and there is no difficulty that in wet in wet chatichah na'asah nevelah does not apply — therefore it appeared that all Mordechai's reasoning is as Beit Yosef wrote precisely: a piece that received recognizable issur taste — the piece; but wet in wet, a blended thing, we do not say this — this is for the view that min b'mino is nullified; but for the view that min b'mino is not nullified — and this is what the Torah revealed regarding blood of the bull and blood of the goat; and although bull blood is more, nevertheless goat blood is upon it — behold on the contrary wet in wet is not nullified; nevertheless the first name is upon it; what is dry in dry we do not say; and if so on the contrary the first name is upon it in its place — and from here to be lenient in wet in wet since the verse revealed explicitly in wet in wet that it is not nullified. And it shall be the first name upon it and it shall be called by the name goat blood, although bull blood is more — therefore Raavad and Mordechai said their words for us who hold min b'mino in wet in wet is nullified, for the first name is uprooted; and they properly wrote: if gravy of issur mixed with permitted, chatichah na'asah nevelah does not apply, for its name is not upon it, for it is blended and its name uprooted; but the whole sugya in the Gemara is for the view that min b'mino is not nullified, and all Tosafot follow this. And likewise this of yayin nesech — they already wrote in Zevachim ch. HaTa'aruvot and likewise in ch. HaKometz that yayin nesech forbids in its species in mashahu, for they ruled thus like R' Yehuda who holds min b'mino is not nullified; and they properly challenged that it should be chatichah na'asah nevelah even in wet in wet, for they stand in importance by Torah law — and no difficulty remains except the challenge from hag'alah of vessels; but in any case requires study what is Tosafot's difficulty from the large yorah — for the parable of the yorah was that it absorbed a kezayit of issur and hag'alah water was fifty log, and behold all became nevelah and the pot returned and absorbed from hag'alah water all nevelah — and what does hag'alah help a second time, it will again become nevelah — end of the challenge; and one may argue that this log of water it absorbed is truly forbidden and full nevelah; but what do you judge in it if it is min b'mino with the second hag'alah water — behold min b'mino is nullified in majority; and if you consider it not its species since it absorbed issur and became forbidden — it is not its species and is called by issur name — it is hard, for it did not absorb only one fiftieth of a kezayit of milk, only each log became nevelah; but nevertheless there is no milk taste, only one fiftieth part; and if so when he returned and put in a third time hag'alah water — if kafeilah tastes this second hag'alah water he will certainly not find issur taste, for there was only one fiftieth part and it is nullified in fifty full log the third — and in not its species we follow all by taste whether much or little, and here no taste is sensed; and one must say all Tosafot's difficulty is for Rav who said there chatichah na'asah nevelah and holds min b'mino in mashahu — and if so these first waters absorbed that became nevelah are not nullified at all in hag'alah water; and Tosafot's difficulty fits well: everything is for the view that min b'mino is not nullified — one cannot distinguish at all between wet and dry — and they properly challenged. And in truth my heart pounds that all Tosafot's difficulty should be entirely Rabbenu Tam's and Rashbam's view; but because Tosafot brought below that waters forbidden from issur are not called issur relative to other waters, only issur name upon them — and if so even for Rav water is called species in not its species and nullified as we said — and therefore one may say that when Tosafot hold gravy forbidden from building is relative to gravy species in not its species — namely for Rava who follows after the name and upon this issur name it is forbidden from its importance, not so for Abaye who follows after taste and certainly their tastes are equal — and if so it is min b'mino; and if so even per Tosafot's view Abaye should be challenged how they hag'alah in the yorah; therefore Tosafot did not explain that per their view it stands — also the challenge stands for Abaye; or one may say Tosafot's difficulty applies to everything — hag'alah is as Tosafot wrote in Pesachim even for a wine vessel for a nazir; and if so in vessels they used gentile water forbidden in benefit — how did they hag'alah for Rav who holds min b'mino is not nullified. Granted for R' Eliezer they hag'alah three times — and although the first time it is not nullified, nevertheless what was absorbed from the first time was not nevelah, and they hag'alah a second time; and if they again became forbidden, nevertheless they were only forbidden from one fiftieth part in a kezayit and it is mashahu — and if so when they returned and hag'alah a third time it cannot forbid, for then it is species in not its species as Rosh wrote; but for Rabbenu Tam forever they absorbed a full kezayit of complete issur and forever it will not be nullified — and in this one may understand Tosafot's words that they preceded in the challenge and said the Torah only forbade a pot beit yomo — for apparently it is superfluous, for either way for the view that all vessels they hag'alah who hold noten ta'am li'fegam forbidden — obviously the difficulty is in place how they hag'alah large yorot; and did they have no remedy and the answer that they left until tomorrow does not apply; but per what I wrote it is satisfactory, for even noten ta'am li'fegam forbidden nevertheless its taste is not equal to its species and it is like species in not its species; and likewise from Tosafot in Avodah Zarah daf 66 — if so it stands for Tosafot that nevertheless min b'mino is not called — therefore Tosafot wrote that they praised hag'alah in Beit Yosef and properly challenged — investigate. And see Mishmeret HaBayit daf 123 that wrote these matters — one should be concerned about gentile water from the holy mouth of Ramban.

And behold from Tosafot Chullin daf 110 s.v. this for a pot that they wrote: and for what I explained that the mishnah deals with water in a pot with meat — if so the first part of the braita that teaches also the kchal tears it, etc., is also with meat, and it is not similar to heart that tears it, etc., that does not deal with meat — for if with meat the meat becomes nevelah and returns and forbids the blood, etc. — end of their words. And it is hard: why specifically with meat — per Rabbenu Tam's explanation even per Rashi's, for in the Gemara we establish it for the pot; and per Rabbenu Tam it is without meat only in water — nevertheless the water becomes nevelah and forbids the heart; and what is special about with meat — see Maharsha who explains explicitly that this challenge is only if it deals with meat; but afterward Tosafot wrote the heart is not the subject and the challenge is even if cooked without meat — and in any case this challenge is specifically with meat; and this matter is not understood at first glance — therefore one must say they hold wet in wet does not become nevelah; and if so blood in water is wet in wet and does not become nevelah; but with meat it becomes nevelah for it is wet in dry — and they properly challenged. And from this it is explained that they hold the distinction between wet and dry — and at first glance they contradict their words on the earlier daf as I wrote; but per what I wrote it is satisfactory; and there is no difficulty whence for Tosafot this distinction between wet and wet — for from this difficulty of the pitcher of water, etc., it does not imply; and it appears the difficulty of Ran from two cups, one terumah and one hullin that they mixed — we say it rose — and why should the water mixed in the terumah cup not become chatichah na'asah nevelah, for it fell only mashahu, for it is only one third part — so Tosafot explicitly in Menachot daf 82 side b that it was only mixture two parts water — see there Tosafot who elaborated; and likewise in Zevachim ch. HaTa'aruvot; and per the above it is satisfactory that here min b'mino is not nullified as Tosafot brought there — if so we properly say wet in wet there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah — investigate.

And behold Peri Chadash s.k. 17 holds in rabbinic issur we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, and brought proof from Rambam regarding chicken meat in milk we do not say thus — see there. And Tosafot regarding kchal implies they hold chatichah na'asah nevelah; and likewise regarding wine that fell into a pit they say chatichah na'asah nevelah even though it is rabbinic; and likewise regarding fish and fowl they salted — they inferred chatichah na'asah nevelah; and likewise fish-sellers chatichah na'asah nevelah even though salting and cooking are rabbinic; and one may say as Kereti wrote: they hold in all issurim chatichah na'asah nevelah from one matter in basar b'chalav — even rabbinic issur we say thus; but Rambam holds like R' Eliezer — specifically in basar b'chalav we say thus, specifically Torah issur that is similar to basar b'chalav, not rabbinic — investigate.

And behold Taz s.k. 16 elaborated from Tur's words that wrote for Rabbenu Tam's view that in other issurim too it becomes nevelah — \\"possible to squeeze out\\" forbids — specifically in noten ta'am when there was not sixty in permitted to nullify the issur absorbed; but if forbidden in mashahu, even if forbidden due to its species that forbids in mashahu, the whole does not become nevelah; and Taz wrote the source Chullin daf 100: once taste entered a piece, chatichah na'asah nevelah and forbids all pieces that are its species; and they challenged why need noten ta'am — even mashahu also; and Rabbenu Tam answered: although it forbids in mashahu, nevertheless chatichah na'asah nevelah does not apply; and from this Taz elaborated to permit chametz on Pesach forbidden in mashahu that it does not return and forbid other pieces in mashahu. And the Rav Shach in Nekudat HaKesef elaborated to challenge and agreed with him, Magen Avraham, etc.; and the main reason: what was forbidden in mashahu does not forbid the one that comes regarding species in its species, since it is only mashahu it is like not its species and does not forbid; but chametz on Pesach forbids even in not its species — again there is no difference between mashahu and noten ta'am — what difference; and he brought proof from Ran that implies like Shach's words; and the truth they came in Ran — but in this proof that Tosafot's intent was not like Shach's intent, for Ran on this challenge \\"why need noten ta'am in the piece — even if it received mashahu it should forbid another in min b'mino,\\" etc., wrote: and the authors strained themselves in its resolution; and it appears to me it is another power of mixture, etc.; and if Rabbenu Tam and R' Eliezer's answer were as Shach thinks, what did Ran innovate until he wrote the predecessors strained themselves in this and it appears to him to answer thus — and per Shach's words these are exactly the early authorities' words and there is no innovation here, only it is clear this did not occur to the early authorities.

However what did he need to bring from Ran — in Rosh it appears more clearly in his words, and these are his words for R' Eliezer's views: since taste did not enter the piece, behold it is as if it does not exist and is not considered min b'mino; but when it received taste it is called min b'mino, etc.; and likewise he wrote afterward: specifically in noten ta'am of issur then it is called min b'mino to forbid other pieces; but if forbidden in mashahu, since one need only nullify the first issur, it is not considered min b'mino in any amount — end of his words; and this teaches like Shach's view.

However this is certainly for R' Eliezer's view that the second piece itself does not forbid, for it did not become nevelah — only what is absorbed inside it; and in this one may well say if the absorption is slight it is like not its species and nullified — and this is what Rosh inferred: since one need only nullify the first issur, it is not considered min b'mino in any amount — behold for Rabbenu Tam who must nullify the second issur that became chatichah na'asah nevelah, it is considered min b'mino; and so is reasonable for Rabbenu Tam who holds chatichah na'asah nevelah in min b'mino — not that one should ask why does it not become nevelah, behold it itself forbids; and whence — or that what is absorbed is not its species as Shach thinks — nevertheless the body of the second piece itself forbids, it is species and species; and if you say what was forbidden cannot forbid — this does not apply at all, for in truth it goes with it — whatever taste there is, since in any case it goes with it as poskim wrote in many places regarding fish salted with meat and the like.

Therefore it is clear there is no reasoning for Rabbenu Tam to say it is not its species; and Shach built Rabbenu Tam's words onto Rabbenu Ephraim's words — and it is not so, for in R' Eliezer's matter who holds the body of the piece does not forbid, only the absorption — in this one may say like Shach's reasoning that the absorption is not its species; but for Rabbenu Tam that the body of the second piece forbids, there is no place for all the above.

And at first glance there was an opening to say according to Rabbenu Tam there is another reasoning: that chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim we learn from basar b'chalav — and if so we have only as said in basar b'chalav: what the Torah said is specifically in noten ta'am and the manner of cooking the Torah forbade — even whatever is in noten ta'am chatichah na'asah nevelah; but if not in noten ta'am, although forbidden from its species generally, it is not similar to basar b'chalav — we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah upon it; and see Ran who wrote many reasonings like this.

But still difficult: Tosafot for Rabbenu Tam from that a pitcher of water fell at the end, etc., and answered that initially a slight thing fell and did not become nevelah — and they challenged: nevertheless \\"possible to squeeze out\\" forbids; and if so that wine is forbidden — how does a pitcher of water help, etc. And difficult either way: if Tosafot hold these are not dependent on each other — even if no chatichah na'asah nevelah, nevertheless \\"possible to squeeze out\\" forbids per Rashba's view — if so Rabbenu Ephraim should also be challenged what does a pitcher of water help at the end, for \\"possible to squeeze out\\" forbids in any case; and if Tosafot hold they are dependent — if so since we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim, we also do not say \\"possible to squeeze out\\" forbids — if so for Rabbenu Tam it stands, for he holds in issur forbidden in mashahu we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, and even \\"possible to squeeze out\\" we do not say; therefore one must say that for Rabbenu Tam we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in mashahu — the reason is not that the issur name does not apply since we do not learn from basar b'chalav, only as I wrote that regarding forbidding others it is as if it does not exist and the forbidden one, etc.; and this is to forbid others; but regarding itself it is equal whether forbidden through noten ta'am or mashahu — nevertheless it is forbidden by Torah; and if so like receiving noten ta'am, \\"possible to squeeze out\\" forbids — so too receiving mashahu regarding that piece itself there is no difference at all; and if nothing discharges from there onward, obviously forbidden.

And at first glance I would bring proof that even for R' Eliezer this reasoning of \\"not its species\\" is not precise, only \\"as if it does not exist\\" is similar — for Tosafot explain \\"why need noten ta'am\\" — even without noten ta'am also; and answer that we say its species rose as if it is not, etc.; and Tosafot explain such as nevelah was two zayitim and shrank on another zayit — and if so one might not estimate except at sixty in gravy — therefore they said since taste entered the piece it received two zayitim — end. And still difficult: why did they give the parable of taste entering a piece — a piece of sixty and put in it taste of a zayit — even if they put only half a zayit, since in min b'mino it is not nullified, that half zayit of gravy forbids until there is sixty against it; and if it is not its species — what of it, behold the piece itself is also not its species relative to the gravy; and still difficult: why \\"since it gave taste,\\" etc. — rather learn that it is entirely lost; and without taste entering it is as if it does not exist — and at first glance this is compelling.

However one may reject: the other pieces without gravy would nullify the absorption that is species in not its species — but this is strained, for behold it returns and is stirred with the nevelah kezayit placed there that was a zayit and a half — why should it be not its species — requires study.

But go and learn what Rashba wrote in Mishmeret HaBayit daf 97: if issur did not give taste in the piece — although for the view that min b'mino is not nullified the piece is forbidden — nevertheless if the piece entered the gravy, sixty is not required even to nullify that absorption, for since it is mashahu it is as if it does not exist — behold he wrote explicitly that sixty is not required against the absorption; why — behold it absorbed issur, and what of it that it is not its species — and because it is not its species it should not require nullification; and Rashba did not mention at all that other pieces entered with it into the gravy until we say they nullify it — only the piece alone entered and from here it should be permitted; and perforce one must say as he wrote himself that mashahu is as if it does not exist — and so Rashba wrote in his novellae; and so it appears at length in Torat HaBayit; and so Ritva wrote in his novellae to Chullin: it appears more correct that Rav holds nothing forbids in mashahu except when it is visible; but when mixed between issur and heter it is not forbidden; and so Orach Chaim HaAroch general rule 24 din 3 wrote: a thing forbidden in mashahu from issur forbidden in mashahu — chatichah na'asah nevelah does not apply — behold he ruled without distinguishing between chametz and other issurim. And if so Taz spoke well, and so Chokhmat Adam — and it is clear he did not intend the remote path Shach spoke in Nekudat HaKesef on erev Pesach after chatzot and Rambam's views. And therefore I wonder at Magen Avraham who wrote on Taz that he exceeded measure to permit what also the Rav Chokhmat Adam, who was among the elder rabbis and student of Maharal of Prague, wrote to permit, and so Orach Chaim explicitly general rule 24 din 3 — end of his words; and the world practices on issur — it appears from the statement \\"chametz\\" they were concerned for Rosh's view as I wrote; one may question Ran's view and Ramban's and Ra'ah in Beit HaBayit that noten ta'am is not precise — even mashahu forbids; and for this branch there is issur, for it emerges from the mouths of sages of the generation; but to lean to permit is clear it is permitted.

And there is some proof for Ran and Shach's words from that we learn: taste and not substance, etc. — for if it is a novelty, even without noten ta'am also; and Rava said the manner of cooking, etc. — learn that if it is a novelty one cannot say in it noten ta'am also, even mashahu also; and if so it is hard: we say for one tanna hag'alah of gentiles is a novelty as explained in the Gemara in Pesachim — if so even without noten ta'am also; and if so gentile vessels cooked in them should be forbidden even without noten ta'am also, for in any case it is a novelty that even a detrimental thing is forbidden — so too even noten ta'am also; and one must say: if so how do you find hag'alah of gentiles — behold taste is absorbed in water and returns and absorbs; and if they hag'alah in a large vessel that has sixty in the water against the gentile absorption, it forbids even without noten ta'am also — how can one hag'alah; only the difficulty is: behold the absorbed thing more than noten ta'am does not forbid its discharge again — and therefore one may say they hag'alah twice; and what of it that first it was absorbed in it — nevertheless afterward hag'alah helps since also the water does not forbid its discharge; and it helps that the first water was only forbidden in mashahu — and therefore one must say like Shach's view that whatever is forbidden in mashahu, whether its species or not its species, even a thing forbidden in mashahu forbids its discharge; if so per the view that hag'alah of gentiles is in mashahu — how do you find it, for forever the water forbids without end, for there is no difference between its species and not its species — requires study, for one may say these are different aspects — requires study.`,
    '5#א': `A drop of milk, etc. So Semak wrote: a drop that fell on the vessel wall — we are in doubt in ch. Dam Chatat whether it steams through the whole vessel or not; therefore when it fell opposite the gravy and there is sixty in the gravy from the drop it is permitted either way — if it steams through the whole vessel it is nullified, and if it does not steam then it should not forbid (meaning if it does not steam through the whole vessel it should not forbid because presumably it steams inward and is as if it fell in the food — so Beit Yosef explains); but when it fell in the empty place, perhaps it steams on the pot wall until near the gravy so there is not sixty from the drop, and that part of the vessel becomes nevelah, and when he pours the food, etc. — end of his words; and per these words they are the Mechaber's words, only abbreviated.`,
    '5#ב': `Only sixty against the drop. Meaning to permit the food, but the pot is nevertheless forbidden and he should pour the food immediately on the other side.`,
    '7#א': `And if the stream did not break, etc. One must say in Rama's words that if the stream did not break it is like iruy; and since the stream broke as above seif 7 and siman 98 seif 10 in the gloss, scraping suffices, for it is not considered iruy since the stream broke — so Terumat HaDeshen there.`,
    '7#ב': `For iruy only forbids kedei kelipah. Therefore even if there is food in the pot where kelipah remains, one need not peel — the pot is like kelipah; and so Maharshal there.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '6#_': `(There in the hagahah seif 7) And if milk spilled, etc. And the food is permitted, for the lower prevails — meaning the pot stands in the place of kelipah; and per our holding that if the kelipah is removed sixty is required against the kelipah — one must say he did not place the pot by the fire afterward, for then it conveys absorption more than kedei kelipah; but if he places the pot afterward by the fire, sixty is required against the kelipah.`,
    '8#_': `But milk of yours, etc. Meaning a candle called kreuzel that burns from rendered-fat milk and is boiling from the fire; but milk of yours boiling in a pot that the pot boiled by the fire — scraping suffices, for it is not considered iruy since the stream broke as above seif 7 and siman 98 seif 10 in the gloss; and so in Terumat HaDeshen there.`,
    '1#_': `(Siman 92 seif 1) A kezayit of meat, etc. From that piece is forbidden — that which the Mechaber ruled without specification that that piece is forbidden implies even if kafeilah tastes it and says there is no milk taste, nevertheless it is forbidden — and why? And one may answer: we are concerned lest at first there was milk taste in it and that piece was already forbidden, and afterward it discharged from it so that now there is no taste at all — nevertheless, since that piece was already forbidden, it does not return to permission, for this deals when he preceded and removed before it discharges. If so, necessarily there was no taste in it, for if so where did the taste in it go — it does not discharge anything before it rests from its boiling. And one cannot say that it does not discharge full taste but nevertheless discharges weak taste. If so, one may answer: perhaps at first there was full taste and now full taste discharged from it and weak taste remained in it, and therefore its taste is not sensed. If so, it is difficult per our words regarding that which in ch. 25 daf 108 side 1 Rav — a kezayit of meat fell into a cauldron of milk, etc., and challenges: it is nevelah milk, and answers when he preceded and removed — absorbs but does not discharge. And per our words it is still difficult: since there was milk inside the meat, if so even if it only discharges weak taste, why does it not forbid the milk in the cauldron? And one must say: since there is only weak taste, again there is no importance of its species here and it is only like not its species and nullified in sixty. And one may answer: nevertheless it is still difficult regarding that which he wrote that if he removed before it rests from boiling we rely on kafeilah's tasting, and it is impossible that weak taste discharges even before it rests from boiling — why is sixty not required; let us be concerned lest weak taste discharged from it, and on weak taste kafeilah does not sense through tasting. And further, both are milk and one cannot recognize the taste. And one must say: certainly only a slight weak taste discharges from it. And that piece is forbidden is because we are concerned lest that piece received milk taste at first — namely congealed such that when a slight taste is removed from it again its taste is not sensed. If so, we are concerned here lest this slight weak thing discharged from it, and for this reason its taste is not sensed — and if so that piece is properly forbidden. Although Rashi explains on the aforementioned daf side 1 that Rav's statement "since noten ta'am in the piece chatichah na'asah nevelah" refers to the mishnah, and the mishnah deals when part of the piece was in the gravy and part outside the gravy — even though he did not stir and cover at the end, nevertheless we forbid the whole piece, and this is because even without stirring and covering, since part is in the gravy, afterward without stirring and covering it discharges from it. And that they do not combine immediately with the gravy is from this piece, since it is not entirely in the gravy but absorbed in that whole piece before it came to the gravy — but afterward it discharged from it to the gravy and forbids the other pieces. And it is clear from the plain meaning that a drop of milk that fell — if there is noten ta'am — forbids even without stirring at all — in the latter part it teaches stirring and covering, implying in the first part it forbids without stirring and covering; and so it appears Taz explains there — and it appears that if there is not in it, permitted even that piece alone, and why be concerned lest there was taste in that piece and now discharged outside as above. Granted if we explain as Shach wrote that if part in gravy and part outside it does not discharge at all to the gravy except through stirring and covering — if so necessarily the mishnah of the milk drop deals that he stirred and covered at the end — if so one may permit the piece's taste before stirring and there was no taste in it. Not so per Taz's explanation of Rashi that the mishnah deals without stirring and covering, and there is no place to forbid the piece's taste — if so it is difficult, etc. And one must say Rashi follows his view that we do not rely on kafeilah except where there is sixty. If so that which implies in the mishnah "if there is noten ta'am" permitted — it follows there is not sixty in that piece either. If so we say: certainly if kafeilah tasted and said there is no taste, since we already hold there is sixty in it, stam there was never any taste at all and it is permitted even that piece, and we do not say in it that there was taste and afterward discharged. And if so for us who hold by law we rely on kafeilah even when there is no sixty — we do not need Rashi's explanation, for otherwise it would be difficult: if there is no taste in it, why should that piece be permitted? If so one should wonder somewhat at Maharshal and his supporters who ruled like Rashi, since they hold kafeilah's tasting without sixty — it was not for them to rule like him, for otherwise the difficulty as stated applies.`,
    '2#_': `(There) But if he did not manage [in time], etc. — forbidden unless there is sixty in it. One may say accordingly on that which is taught in Chullin 94: a pot in which he cooked meat — he may not cook milk in it; and if he cooked and there is noten ta'am, and we say: who tastes — kafeilah tastes for him. And per what the Mechaber wrote, why do we rely on kafeilah? Let us say milk was absorbed in the pot and noten ta'am and returned and discharged it and forbids the rest of the milk, and it is min b'mino with the rest of the milk and would require sixty as here. And one may say: behold Ran explains that which challenges on that which Rav said: a kezayit of meat fell into a cauldron of milk, etc. — it is nevelah milk; and Ran asks: why noten ta'am? For since this milk that was absorbed is still connected to this milk that is outside it, which is sixty against the taste of meat — the taste of meat that this absorbed milk received was nullified. And one may say: since it was absorbed inside the meat it is immediately separated from the rest of the milk and stands alone, and properly received meat taste and noten ta'am. And behold per what we hold: if issur was absorbed in food, the issur cannot be squeezed out, for the issur cannot discharge from it; but if issur was absorbed in a vessel we say hag'alah helps and the issur can properly separate from it — and this is the reason: in absorption in food the issur clings to it and adheres more and is absorbed literally in the substance until it cannot give space between the adhesions forever; not so in absorption in a vessel, which does not cling and adhere so much with the vessel, and therefore in hag'alah it can properly separate from it. Accordingly one may say that which we say: if something was absorbed into something else it is considered as separated from what is outside it — this is if absorbed into the food as above, and it is all one body, and we properly say it is as separating and noten ta'am; not so what clings in a vessel, which does not cling so much with the vessel — we say it is still connected to the food inside the vessel, and we do not say that since it was absorbed it is noten ta'am. Accordingly it works out [and so too Peri Megadim at the beginning of siman 94 in Sha'ar HaTevunah this distinction in name of Mordechai]: for here, which deals with absorption inside the vessel, we say noten ta'am and properly requires sixty; not so there regarding a pot in which he cooked, etc. — for since it was absorbed in the vessel we do not say it should be as separated from meat and be noten ta'am and need no sixty at all to nullify it. But still difficult is that which the Mechaber wrote above at the end of seif 4: if he placed [milk] in the hide of the stomach — if there is noten ta'am in it, it is forbidden; it implies if there is no noten ta'am it is permitted even when there is no sixty. Let us say the milk absorbed in the stomach hide is noten ta'am and returned and discharged and would need sixty to nullify — there too it was absorbed in food. And one must say: since it is only forbidden through pickling, and from the Torah it is not forbidden at all in basar b'chalav except through cooking — the Mechaber holds we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, because he holds like Rabbenu Efraim that we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah except in basar b'chalav — and this is because since this alone is permitted, etc., only by their joining together it became forbidden — it is properly as one body of nevelah; and all this if forbidden through cooking, which is as a body of issur; not so through pickling and salting — even though they forbade eating, nevertheless we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah that the whole is considered one body, only it is considered like other issurim. [Hagahah: and so too in the Gra's chiddushim at the end of seif 7, that for Taz in siman 90 s.k. 4 that the Mechaber holds in basar b'chalav that is d'rabbanan we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah — it works out.]`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 2 in the hagahah) And likewise if he did not stir, etc. Shach s.k. 6 wants to explain Rama's words that he is uncertain whether we hold like R' Yehuda or like Rashi, etc. — see there; and this is that he follows his view that he holds below s.k. 3 that Rashi holds: if part is in the gravy and part outside the gravy, it does not spread at all without stirring and covering. But as appears from Taz s.k. 5 who explains Rashi's words: if part of it, etc. — it is forbidden — this is because it absorbed before the absorbed reached other pieces and gravy; but nevertheless, since part is in the gravy, it returns and discharges into it afterward issur and forbids even without stirring and covering — see there. And behold it appears proven like Shach: behold Tosafot in ch. Gid HaNasheh daf 93 side 2 s.v. im yesh bah noten ta'am letter 5: when he stirred the pot, the drop is conveyed throughout the pot and everything inside helps nullify the drop; and likewise when he covered the pot, the boiling rises from the sides and conveys the drop throughout the pot — it implies he means even the piece on which the drop fell was partly in the gravy — if he did not stir and cover, the gravy and pieces do not help nullify it. And for R' Yehuda it appears: when the piece on which issur fell is partly in the gravy, he does not require stirring and covering; and there it deals when it is entirely outside the gravy — then issur does not spread except through stirring and covering, etc. — see there. And it appears explicitly from their words that for Rashi, that which Rabbi said: the words of R' Yehuda appear correct without stirring and without covering — chatichah na'asah nevelah — deals with part in gravy and part outside gravy; and on this the Gemara challenges: if we say without stirring and covering at all — absorbs absorbs, discharges does not discharge — behold explicitly for Rashi the piece does not forbid what is in the pot at all if he did not stir, as Shach's view. However from the Gemara in Chullin daf 100 it appears the opposite, as Taz's view, from that which we say there: since there is noten ta'am in the piece — chatichah na'asah nevelah; and Tosafot wrote there s.v. chatichah na'asah nevelah letter 5: they were cooked first before he put other pieces in the pot, and there was no sixty in the issur, and the nevelah imparted taste in it, etc. — and it challenges the explanation: if so, the gravy too that was at that hour was first forbidden through the taste absorbed, and shortly it implies they nullify, etc. — see there. Behold per Shach's view in Rashi's words it works out: perhaps it deals in first cooking — part was in gravy and part outside gravy — this does not discharge at all to the gravy and the gravy was not forbidden; or not like Shach — even in such a case it discharges, and Tosafot's challenge is well; also from Rashi himself in the sugya of the milk drop daf 108 it appears not like Shach — for Rashi wrote there s.v. amar Rav, etc.: that which the mishnah taught "if there is noten ta'am in it," etc. — in that piece, and it forbids the whole piece, etc. — if so Rav refers to the mishnah, and the plain meaning of the mishnah implies the first part deals without stirring and covering at all, and even so it forbids the rest of the pot; if so it is proven as Taz's words: if part is in the gravy, nevertheless it discharges and forbids other pieces. And one must say certainly the truth is with Taz. And that which Tosafot wrote in ch. Gid HaNasheh that it appears he means even what fell on it — the drop — is partly in the gravy, etc. — which in haste appears they explain according to Rashi, that Rabbi's "the words of R' Yehuda appear" deals with part in gravy — if so per Taz's view the challenge from absorbs absorbs, discharges does not discharge, etc., does not apply at all. One may say: certainly Tosafot explain that Rashi also establishes Rabbi's "appear correct," etc., that it deals entirely outside the gravy; and that which Tosafot wrote "and it appears to me" that even the piece on which it fell was partly in — this is that Rashi holds this law: the piece itself is forbidden and other pieces do not combine to nullify the tereifah; if so there is no challenge: why did Rabbi say "appear correct," etc., with stirring, etc., and he did not say "the words of the Sages appear" with part in gravy — because in such a case he truly holds like R' Yehuda that we say chatichah na'asah nevelah and forbids also the other pieces; not so per R' Yehuda's view — in such a case other pieces also combine — it should have said "the words of the Sages appear" with part in gravy. And one may say per Taz's view: that which Tosafot challenged for R' Yehuda's view — it should have said "the words of the Sages appear" with part in gravy — does not challenge per Rashi's view, that which Rabbi said "appear correct," etc., without stirring at first but at the end — it should establish Rabbi's words that he did not stir and cover at all, and it deals with part in gravy, etc. [Hagahah: and Peleti here also raised on this from Taz's words and wrote requires study to resolve it with difficulty. End of hagahah.] And one may resolve: behold certainly the dispute of R' Yehuda and the Sages is in every case — for R' Yehuda, even if he stirred from beginning to end, it is also forbidden; and the Sages disagree that in every matter it is not forbidden, whether issur fell on the piece with part in gravy or entirely outside gravy — permitted; and R' Yehuda disagrees with them and forbids all that they permitted. And even though he said "the words of R' Yehuda appear correct" without stirring — chatichah na'asah nevelah — it implies in all cases they disagree; he holds like R' Yehuda without stirring, and even entirely outside gravy — and on this he challenges well: if he did not stir at all — absorbs absorbs, etc. And he needed to resolve that he means he did not stir at the beginning but at the end — this in every manner forbids the rest of the pot. And accordingly Taz's view stands, and Shach's challenge returns to Rama in its place in that which he wrote: that piece is forbidden and also requires sixty against the drop — if so necessarily he is concerned for Rashi's view — if so he should have said one requires sixty against the whole piece, for it discharges even without stirring at all. And one must say Rama deals when it is entirely outside the gravy; and that which requires sixty against the drop — this is because it is not clear to him whether the steam of the pot does not convey throughout — for one may say the steam of the pot conveys taste throughout the pot, and if so that piece is forbidden lest it does not convey, and he also needs sixty in the pot against the drop lest it conveys throughout. And accordingly one must say that which Tosafot wrote in ch. Gid HaNasheh daf 100 s.v. chatichah na'asah nevelah — rather one may say it deals such as: there is a permitted piece entirely outside the gravy, and a nevelah piece is placed on it outside the gravy, and the permitted piece became nevelah because it absorbs nevelah taste that has no sixty to nullify the taste; nevertheless when he stirred the pot it forbids all the pieces because it is its species — end of his words. One must say Tosafot's view is it deals after he removed the issur he then added other pieces; for otherwise, if it deals that even before he removed the nevelah the pieces were in the pot — if so it is difficult: why need it impart taste in the pieces? For even without imparting taste the whole pot is forbidden, lest the steam of the pot conveys throughout — if so it is an amount of the nevelah itself that forbids other pieces. For granted one may say: if placed entirely outside the gravy, certainly it does not convey throughout, only that piece — if so it must impart taste in the piece, for otherwise it is two amounts and we do not say two amounts; not so now that we say the steam of the pot conveys throughout — if so this amount of the nevelah itself forbids other pieces, and there is no place to say two amounts. Or it deals that it was cooked first without other pieces — only in first cooking it was placed entirely outside gravy, which is not so for Rashi who does not establish such a case — the challenge is well that the gravy was forbidden. And one may say: if so per their view too, why was the gravy not forbidden, lest the steam of the pot conveys throughout? One may say it deals when there was sixty in the gravy against the nevelah itself — if so in any case the gravy is permitted: if the steam of the pot conveys throughout — when the nevelah fell there it conveyed throughout the pot and the taste was nullified in the gravy; and if it does not convey throughout — then it did not discharge its taste at all to the gravy. Not so that piece forbids other pieces, lest it does not convey throughout. Not so per Rashi's view — the challenge is well in any case how it deals: if placed entirely inside the gravy — if so necessarily there is no sixty in the gravy, for if there were sixty in the gravy against the nevelah, this piece too would not be forbidden at all. One reason: we say it removed its species, etc.; and further, even though he certainly removed, etc., nevertheless since this piece is only forbidden due to an amount, there is no place to forbid other pieces — for it is two amounts. And if it deals that it was placed part in gravy — well this piece that is placed on it is forbidden even though there is sixty in the gravy against the nevelah — for in such a case there is no place to say it removed its species and stands in its forbidden state; behold per Taz's view that stands with us, he holds since part is in gravy it discharges even without stirring and covering — if so even though there is sixty in the gravy against the nevelah, nevertheless against the whole piece there is no sixty, and if so the gravy is forbidden from the piece since there is no sixty against it — and Tosafot challenge Rashi well — investigate. And behold the author of Sefer Pri To'ar wrote one may say Rashi holds: that which that piece is forbidden is what is above the gravy, where the power of the gravy does not reach, and part is forbidden; but what is inside the gravy is not forbidden, for immediately when absorption comes there the gravy combines to nullify it, since part is in the gravy — what is above the gravy does not harm what is inside — see there. And from Tosafot the opposite appears, who wrote ch. Gid HaNasheh daf 90 side 2 s.v. im yesh bah noten ta'am: per Rashi's view, "if there is noten ta'am in it" specifically — he holds that if part is in the gravy, etc., that piece is forbidden alone and the gravy does not combine; but for Rashi one must say "if there is noten ta'am in it" is not specific — and it cannot be as Pri To'ar wrote, for also for Rashi one must say it is not specific, for it appears in the mishnah: since there is in the sinew the measure to impart taste in that thigh — behold it is forbidden, meaning the whole thigh. And per what Pri To'ar wrote, that part placed inside the gravy — the gravy combines to nullify — if so also for Rashi one must say it is not specific when he said "if there is in it," rather certainly for Rashi the whole piece is forbidden — investigate.`,
    '4#_': `(There in the hagahah) And some say we state chatichah na'asah nevelah in all issurim. And behold Ran wrote: and so most authors — that even per Rabbenu Tam's view that in other issurim we say chatichah na'asah nevelah, it is only d'rabbanan because of the taste of basar b'chalav. And it is difficult accordingly: that which Tosafot challenged in ch. 25 daf 100 s.v. keshekadim vesalku, etc., per Rabbenu Tam's explanation — how did they hag'alah the large yorot, since the water is noten ta'am, etc. — see there. And per what the commentators said, well they hag'alahed it from the Torah, and there is no chatichah na'asah nevelah at all in other issurim. And some want to resolve that certainly Tosafot also hold it is only d'rabbanan, and that which they challenged from the large yorah — this is since we find explicit permission in the Torah, the Sages would not forbid what the Torah explicitly permitted to the seeing eye — investigate. And in my humble opinion it appears thus: behold Tosafot challenged Rabbenu Tam's view from the large yorah — this is specifically per Rabbenu Tam who holds that only taste is d'oraisa up to sixty; if so, well one may say chatichah na'asah nevelah is forbidden from the Torah in all issurim; not so per Rabbenu Tam the Kohen who on the view of noten ta'am one is not lashed unless there is a kezayit in the time to eat a peras — if so, certainly for him chatichah na'asah nevelah is d'rabbanan; and we hold like R' Chaim Kohen who on taste one is not lashed — if so, certainly chatichah na'asah nevelah is d'rabbanan. [And as he wrote in his book Kereti uPeleti.] And one may say: Tosafot's challenge is even per R' Chaim Kohen — for even though he holds that for more than the time to eat a peras one is not lashed, nevertheless all this is in min b'mino; not so min b'mino according to Rav who holds min b'mino with any amount — nevertheless we certainly say chatichah na'asah nevelah according to all — and if so Tosafot challenged well: if so in hag'alah of the law too there is concern lest he used it on a day of wine offered to idolatry, which is min b'mino with hag'alah, and we say each time chatichah na'asah nevelah. And it is fine per R' Eliezer's view who holds we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim — if so, well they could hag'alah the water of issur absorbed in vessels: a quarter-log of water and the vessel holds thirty zayitim — if so, if they hag'alah it the first time and the water removed the issur and it was absorbed afterward in the vessel — if so it only absorbed a thirtieth of a zayit of issur; for even though what is absorbed now is also a zayit like what was first absorbed, nevertheless since it fell into hag'alah water and returns and absorbs from hag'alah water, it is absorption for the view that parts of the permitted water and one part of issur — if so when hag'alahed a second time there is no noten ta'am at all in the second hag'alah water, only the second hag'alah water is forbidden in any amount, which is the second hag'alah water that is also boiling; and if the vessel holds less than that, such that even in the second hag'alah there is noten ta'am in the water — hag'alah three times, and each time according to the size of what was absorbed and the smallness of the vessel's capacity — increase hag'ala until what was absorbed remains without noten ta'am. Not so Rabbenu Tam who holds chatichah na'asah nevelah also in other issurim — if so when the water was first forbidden up to noten ta'am the water became nevelah; if so even if they hag'alahed a hundred times it does not help, for each time we say in it chatichah na'asah nevelah, and each time what is absorbed absorbs a kezayit of issur as before, since there is no sixty at all in hag'alah water against what was absorbed from it, and the water becomes like issur of nevelah — if so Tosafot challenged well according to Rav who holds min b'mino in any amount — investigate well. [Hagahah of the great Rav, etc., R' Shlomo Kohen, may his light shine, first chapter Vilna. The Gaon's words here are astonishing in my humble opinion, for the matter is plain that min b'mino according to Rav, even though it is from the Torah — for we derive from "and he shall take" the blood of the bull and the blood of the goat — nevertheless lashes certainly do not apply as long as there is no kezayit in the time to eat a peras, for it is no greater than min in not its species; for us, for R' Chaim Kohen there are no lashes on taste, and since min in not its species for us chatichah na'asah nevelah is only d'rabbanan, as written above in name of Peleti — per Rabbenu Tam's view it is plain that the same applies to min b'mino according to Rav; if so necessarily Tosafot's challenge is only to Rabbenu Tam, as Peleti concludes. And incidentally, since we deal with the law of chatichah na'asah nevelah, I saw fit to write on what Peri Megadim was uncertain in Mishbetzot Zahav siman 100 s.k. 1 regarding things that forbid above sixty, such as chametz and spice, etc. — whether we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in them; and he wrote that from Rabbenu Tam's words in Tur siman 92 it is explained explicitly that we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in a thing forbidden in any amount. However from Ran in ch. 25 who challenged there from that which we taught in the first chapter of Temurah that the diluted mixture does not return to dilute except by calculation, etc., and he needed to resolve that in dry in dry they did not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim because it is not similar to basar b'chalav — see there; in Ran it appears that in wet in wet, even above sixty, we say chatichah na'asah nevelah — so too Peri Megadim there. However in my humble opinion it appears one may say Ran follows his view that he holds chametz and spice and the like forbid from the Torah above sixty, as Peri Megadim wrote in siman 98 in Sha'ar HaTevunah s.k. 29 in his name, and so too there the Gra in the Yoreh De'ah margin — if so it is not less than other issurim that forbid up to sixty, regarding which they decreed chatichah na'asah nevelah because of the taste of basar b'chalav, as Ran wrote. But per what Shach wrote in siman 98 s.k. 29 in name of Issur VeHeter explicitly that it is only d'rabbanan — certainly one may say we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in them; not to mention per Rambam and Mechaber in siman 90 regarding basar b'chalav itself where it is only d'rabbanan, such as milk of a slaughtered animal and the like, that we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, as Taz wrote there per their view — how much more so in other d'rabbanan issurim or in a d'rabbanan mixture; but even for us who do not hold thus, nevertheless above sixty one may certainly say we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah, since the main reason we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim is because of the taste of meat and milk, and in basar b'chalav we do not find that it forbids above sixty from the Torah — and see Shach in this siman s.k. 15 what he wondered about Afiei Revavai. And in my humble opinion it appears Afiei Revavai follows Maharshal's view that Taz brought in s.k. 15: in other issurim themselves, that we say in wet in wet we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah — specifically when cold mixed with cold and there is no sixty; but hot in hot through cooking, where the issur gives wet taste in the heter — also in other issurim we say chatichah na'asah nevelah — see there. And per this, that which Rama wrote in the hagahah in basar b'chalav one may not rely on this — also cold in cold deals with it; if so, Afiei Revavai wrote well that it is only a d'rabbanan stringency, for from the Gemara that we say "it is nevelah milk" there is no proof at all, for there it is hot in hot and through taste that the piece imparted taste in milk — in such a case Afiei Revavai concedes it is forbidden from the Torah in basar b'chalav; but cold in cold one may well say that also in basar b'chalav it is only from stringency as above — to resolve Afiei Revavai's words. And so one must explain Darkei Moshe's words in siman 99 letter 2 in that which he wrote there to resolve Tur's words per Rashba's view in his responsum that he distinguishes and wrote: specifically if cold mixed with cold or dry with dry — then we say that if he increased upon them unintentionally they are permitted, etc. And it appears Tur wrote stam all issurim, etc., and it implies he deals even with basar b'chalav, as Darkei Moshe himself concludes there; and in basar b'chalav it is explained explicitly in the Gemara that even cold in cold chatichah na'asah nevelah is from the Torah, for we challenge "it is nevelah milk" — if so necessarily it deals with cold and cold. However in truth Tur's language there implies he deals with a hot dish into which issur fell — and then Darkei Moshe's explanation there cannot stand at all, and still requires study. End of hagahah of the great Rav, etc., R' Shlomo Kohen, may his light shine, first chapter Vilna.]`,
    '5#_': `(Seif 5) A drop of milk that fell on the pot, etc. This law has its source in Semag, and these are his words: a drop that fell on the wall of the vessel — we are in doubt in ch. Dam Chatat whether it steams through the whole vessel or not, etc. And see Shach s.k. 18 who brought his wording. And behold in Semag's doubt one may say two explanations, and these are the Gemara's words in ch. Dam Chatat daf 93: they asked — partial cooking in a vessel requires rinsing and washing in the whole vessel or not? He said: it does not require; it is reasonable: as with a garment — as a garment requires laundering only where there is blood, so too a vessel requires rinsing and washing only where there was cooking. He said: are they comparable? Blood does not steam; cooking steams. And further it teaches: rinsing and washing are more stringent than sprinkling — partial cooking in a vessel requires rinsing and washing in the whole vessel. He said: if it teaches, it teaches, etc. — perhaps I would include even terumah; the verse says "it" — to exclude terumah. And it challenges: terumah does not require rinsing and washing, yet it teaches: a pot in which he cooked terumah — he may not cook non-sacred food in it; and if he cooked and there is noten ta'am — Abaye said: it was not needed, for the Master said partial cooking in a vessel requires rinsing and washing in the whole vessel. And Rashba explains: certainly if he cooked in part of the vessel it steams through the whole vessel, only it does not steam above the place where he cooked — only weak taste — and this was the stringency of chatat that even above requires rinsing to remove weak taste. If so one may say this law of Semag: certainly it cannot be that it steams through the whole vessel, only it is doubtful to him whether the explanation is like Rashba — if so, even nevelah in the empty place combines the dish to nullify all that steams; if the explanation is as plain meaning — that "it" comes to exclude from terumah, meaning it does not steam at all, only in chatat it is a gezeirat hakatuv that it requires rinsing and washing, and one might say the same for terumah — therefore the Merciful One wrote "it" — to exclude terumah. Accordingly, if we say like Rashba's explanation, he need not wait only until it cools, for it is already nullified; but if it cannot be that it does not steam at all — if so the drop stands in its place and it is forbidden to pour on that side, or he must wait until it cools. And that which Semag wrote: but when it fell in the empty place, perhaps it steams to the wall of the pot near the food — so too that there is no sixty against the drop and that part becomes nevelah, etc. — this is where the wall of the vessel is thick, such that if he pours the dish by way of the wall of the vessel where the drop fell there will not be sixty in the dish — then it forbids the dish. And all Semag's doubt is whether it steams along the length or width of the vessel; or one may say this is Semag's doubt — and so is the meaning of all Acharonim in his view who explain Semag's words thus: this is certain — partial cooking steams above the place of cooking; only it is doubtful to him whether it steams through the whole vessel or only part of the vessel. And if it steams through the whole vessel — then the absorption reaches the dish too and is nullified in sixty; but if it cannot be that it does not steam through the whole vessel — then one must be concerned lest it steams on the wall of the vessel along length and width until there is no sixty against the drop and chatichah na'asah nevelah and forbids the dish afterward when he pours from it; therefore he must wait until it cools. And accordingly, even if the wall of the vessel is very thin, nevertheless it is forbidden to pour from it — and so Shach s.k. 23 who wrote, and these are his words: and even though it appears it is not comparable to a spoon, etc., nevertheless since you come to forbid the wall of the pot because of the drop — necessarily one must say lest cooking does not steam through the whole vessel, etc. — end of his words. It appears from his words that he explains the second explanation: for if the first explanation, even if the dish steams, in any case it is not forbidden, for it only steams weak taste; or he holds the second explanation that it steams full taste.`,
    '7#_': `(There) And splashing from a boiling pot, etc. It is like kli sheni — meaning he does not even need kelipah; and even though we hold hot meat that fell into cold milk requires kelipah — when it is absorbed it absorbs even though the stream broke — one may distinguish between thick and thin. However one may say: it appears from the mishnah of dripping from their gravy onto the earthenware and it returned to it — remove its place — even though it is a thin thing, and even so it is forbidden because of the earthenware, and even though the stream broke. But it works out, for that which Rama wrote: if the stream broke with a thin thing, it only forbids even in kedei kelipah — this is for us who hold the lower prevails; if so, since the lower is cold it cools the upper, and therefore if the stream broke it does not absorb at all, even in kedei kelipah — how much more so for the view that the upper prevails: even when the stream broke it also absorbs, for there is nothing here to cool it. Accordingly it works out for the view that the lower prevails — in any case the Gemara there establishes it with hot earthenware; and for the view that the upper prevails — it establishes it with cold earthenware — still it works out for us that even when the stream broke it also cooks. However from Maharsha's words there it appears that even for the view that the lower prevails, even a thin thing and the stream broke also absorbs in it — as he challenged: that which Tosafot wrote there regarding dripping from its gravy onto the batter — we do not have the text "remove its place," for we hold the lower prevails, and his intent was to say: when it is absorbed it absorbs; or he holds even when the stream broke with a thin thing, when it is absorbed it absorbs — and not like Rama.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '3#_': `(There seif 3) And if he cooked it with others, sixty is required to nullify all of it; and if he recognizes it he throws it away and the others are permitted. And know that we also require majority that the majority of pieces be permitted, as the law of dry in dry — siman 109 — see Shach there; and see siman 102. And this is Shach's intent when he wrote: and if it is not fit to be honored it is nullified in majority — meaning we require majority in any case.`,
    '6#_': `There seif 6. Custom permits through cooling — see Shach and poskim on when the pot remains forbidden.`,
    '8#_': `There seif 8. Two pots do not forbid each other — applied to panada oven and meat pot, as Taz wrote.`,
    '9#_': `There seif 9. Milk candle from the wick side — not from other side of vessel; Maharam on hot drop on cold knife — scraping; Maharil on iruy from kli rishon only.`,
    '1#_': `(Siman 92 seif 2 in the hagahah) A drop of milk that fell on a piece in the pot that is outside the gravy, etc. If he did not stir at all, only that piece is forbidden — so Beit Yosef in name of Raavad, and so is proven explicitly in Tosafot and Rosh in ch. Gid HaNasheh (daf 100) regarding that which he said: a piece of nevelah and of unclean fish do not forbid until they impart taste in the gravy, etc. — and they wrote it deals when the permitted piece is placed entirely outside the gravy and on it is placed the nevelah piece, and only that piece became forbidden; and afterward when he stirred the pot, the whole became forbidden — end of his words. It is proven explicitly that without stirring only that piece was forbidden. And in Darkei Moshe he is uncertain whether this is specifically for R' Yehuda, which deals when the piece is entirely outside the gravy; but for Rashi, which deals when the piece on which the drop fell is partly in the gravy — other pieces are forbidden even without stirring at all. And he inferred from Tur's words in this siman a weak inference that the same applies to Rashi — without stirring at all other pieces in the pot are not forbidden. And Taz wrote explicitly that for Rashi, which deals when part is in the gravy, other pieces are forbidden even without stirring. And it was hidden from them Rashba's words in Torat HaKahanim and Taz siman 105 seif 4, and it is explained from their words explicitly that the same applies to Rashi who wrote, and these are his words: if issur fell on a piece in the pot that is outside the gravy and he did not stir the pot, it only forbids kedei netilah; but if it is in the gravy — for Rashi entirely, and for R' Yehuda even part of it — the gravy steams the taste and it enters throughout — end of his words. Behold it is explained that for Rashi, if part of it — the gravy does not steam without stirring; and this is even if it is in the gravy, meaning even from that netilah that was forbidden — only its upper face is exposed, as Beit Yosef wrote — investigate well. And see Rashba brought in Beit Yosef siman 66. And so it appears to me proven in Rashi's explanation in ch. Kol HaBasar (daf 108 end of side 2) who explained on that which is said in the Gemara there: if he did not stir at all and did not cover at all — absorbs absorbs, discharges does not discharge. Rashi: the milk is absorbed in the piece but is not discharged from it to forbid other pieces without stirring — for the milk is on the roof of the piece — end of his words. It implies it deals when the piece is inside the gravy, only that its roof, meaning its upper face is exposed, where the drop fell and where it stands, and it does not spread from it outward without stirring. And so Hagahot Maimoniyot, brought by Beit Yosef, and these are his words: from Rashi's explanation it appears, etc., and as Beit Yosef explained his words — see there. And it appears to me that from these words of Rashi he derived thus: the same applies regarding combination of other pieces to nullify the drop — they do not combine even if it is in the gravy if its upper face is exposed; for all the more so if it does not forbid other pieces, it does not combine to nullify. And see Shach s.k. 6 and s.k. 13.`,
    '2#_': `(There in Shulchan Aruch) And likewise if it fell into the gravy, etc. This is Rambam's language. And Tur wondered at him: why need to stir when it fell into the gravy? And it appears to me Rambam deals with thick gravy, as we establish the mishnah in the Gemara in ch. Kol HaBasar (daf 108) for Rav who holds min b'mino is not nullified; and that the braita does not challenge is because he did not hear it, as Tosafot wrote there — and this answer is primary, as written in chiddushim in Sha'ar HaTevunah. And it appears this is R' Yehuda's reason in the Gemara there (daf 109) who holds that even if he stirred immediately, other pieces do not combine to nullify the drop — for he is concerned lest he did not stir well, and that piece on which the drop fell became nevelah and forbids all the pieces because they are its species; for R' Yehuda holds min b'mino is not nullified, and they did not explain why he is concerned lest he did not stir well. And in my humble opinion necessarily R' Yehuda deals with thick gravy, as we establish for Rav there — for if thin gravy, it would be not its species relative to meat, and we say it removed its species, etc., and min in not its species is great upon it and nullifies it — and why does R' Yehuda say it forbids all the pieces because they are its species? If so, since he deals with thick gravy, therefore he is concerned lest he did not stir well well, since it is thick. If so, similar to this the Sages of R' Yehuda also deal with this, and hold stirring helps, for they are not concerned lest he did not stir well; however stirring certainly is required since the gravy is thick — investigate. And it appears to me clear and correct in Sha'ar HaTevunah. And see Beit Yosef who challenged Rambam who wrote "he stirred the pot" — behold we do not nullify issur l'chatchila. And one may say he holds this is not nullification, for he only mixes; and this reasoning Tosafot wrote in ch. 4 of Terumot mishnah 8, and Peri Chadash brought in Orach Chaim siman 453. And greater still Magen Avraham at the beginning of siman Shelah wrote: if his intent is not to eat the issur but only to save the heter through its nullification, it is not called nullifying issur; and if so here too his intent is not to eat the drop of milk but only that the whole meat not become forbidden — and such a case I found in responsum of Maharam of Rothenburg siman 190 — see there. And I saw Peri Megadim here and in Orach Chaim challenged Peri Chadash who brought proof from the mishnah there, which deals only for R' Yehuda and we do not hold like him, as Rambam wrote; and so he challenged in responsum of R' Akiva Eiger siman 77 and did not mention Peri Megadim preceded him. And further he challenged: if so that grinding is permitted since his intent is only to fix — why are basar b'chalav not nullified — slaughter them in order to eat them and they will be nullified by themselves? And so with pomegranates — one may wound them for fixing food — what difference between grinding wheat and wounding pomegranates? And one may say grinding is forbidden since everything mixed together — it is as intending to nullify. And in the end one may say wheat is different, that he grinds everything together — if so there is also permission here. Not so with slaughter of basar b'chalav and wounding pomegranates, where the way is to do one by one, and on each one there is doubt lest it is the issur — his intent is not to fix. Rather he challenged one difficulty: what the Yerushalmi implies, that we only permit to permit regarding meduma because the priests will eventually grind them — and why permit to grind wheat that has shoots? Requires study. And one must say: the reason is still permission in any case. And accordingly there is no contradiction to our master's words here, that he mixes the whole pot together before it imparts taste — it is literally like grinding wheat — requires study. And further I have to say the Yerushalmi that implies forbidding grinding wheat in other issurim is because he considers it intent to nullify, since it is pikuach nefesh. And this is only for the view that nullifying issur is from the Torah forbidden; but per what we hold it is only d'rabbanan — he is not considered as having intent due to pikuach nefesh, for we permit in d'rabbanan.`,
    '4#_': `(Seif 4 in the hagahah) That we say in all issurim chatichah na'asah nevelah. It is explained in Ran end of ch. Kol HaBasar that it is only rabbinic due to the gezeirah of basar b'chalav — see there — and so the Acharonim. And it appears to me that a kezayit within the time to eat a peras — per Rabbenu Tam one would be lashed for every kezayit of the mixture, for all the permitted became issur. As Rosh wrote ch. 3 and Taz siman 98. If so, necessarily chatichah na'asah nevelah is d'oraisa. And it is proven explicitly in Tosafot and Rosh and Ran ch. 3 (daf 100) and Mordechai there siman 448 — that even for R' Efraim, whom Taz ruled we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim except basar b'chalav — nevertheless for a kezayit within the time to eat a peras he concedes. For they wrote that therefore in basar b'chalav R' Eliezer holds chatichah na'asah nevelah. And one must estimate against the whole piece on which the drop fell, and it does not suffice to nullify the drop alone. For in basar b'chalav the whole mixture becomes issur, for one is lashed on half a kezayit of meat and half a kezayit of milk as stated in the Gemara — see there — it is proven explicitly that the same applies in all other issurim where the whole mixture becomes issur and one is lashed on every kezayit from it — namely within the time to eat a peras — we properly say chatichah na'asah nevelah and need to estimate sixty against the whole mixture for all views. And this is clear.`,
    '5#_': `(There in Shach s.k. 16) And regarding whether we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in salting, etc. — there is room to permit for great loss, etc., and so is primary. And he explained the reason well in his long book that I brought siman 105. And Mahari Weil general rule 38 s.k. 5 did not see that book, and therefore attacked him for nothing. And this — that he did not see his long book — also caused him to leave Darkei Moshe's words in siman 105 seif 8 in requires study, for it implies he is lenient also in roasting. And per what he wrote there, one may be lenient also in roasting as I wrote there. Tur wrote per Rabbenu Tam's view who holds we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim and one must estimate sixty against the whole piece — specifically what was forbidden through noten ta'am; but what was forbidden in any amount did not become all nevelah — end of his words. It appears to me proven explicitly in Tosafot and Rosh in ch. Gid HaNasheh (daf 100) and Ran in ch. Kol HaBasar and in Rosh ch. 2 of Avodat Kochavim and Tur and Shulchan Aruch siman 134 seif 4: a thing forbidden due to any amount does not become nevelah. And if it afterward fell in other heter, one need not estimate except according to the measure of the first issur. And even wet in wet that mixed well — behold there is here literally an amount of the first issur. Even so one need not estimate against the whole mixture. And it is not comparable to what Taz wrote regarding chametz on Pesach, that we say two amounts in wet in wet — for there it is different, for also in this mixture the first issur forbids in any amount, and behold there is here an amount from it; but if in this mixture the first issur does not forbid in any amount but in noten ta'am, one needs that it impart taste in it. And so is proven explicitly in Asheri ch. 2 of Avodat Kochavim regarding wine offered to idolatry that fell into a pit of permitted wine — it was forbidden in any amount, and afterward a pitcher of water fell there — we say it removed its species as it is not, and min in not its species is great upon it and nullifies it. And Rosh wrote, and these are his words: and that which we do not say here chatichah na'asah nevelah and water would need to nullify permitted wine and forbidden wine — because it did not fall from the forbidden wine in the measure of imparting taste in the heter, only in any amount, and every thing forbidden in any amount — we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah — end of his words. Beit Yosef brings it in this siman and siman 134. And so in Shulchan Aruch there seif 4 — see there. And there is practical difference regarding a thing that has permitters, that forbids in its species in any amount and not in its species in noten ta'am, as I wrote at the beginning of siman 102. And if it mixed in its species and was forbidden in any amount, and this mixture mixed in not its species whose measure is noten ta'am — the first issur must impart taste in it, and one need not estimate against the whole mixture, only according to the calculation of the first issur in it. However, if the mixture remains visibly in its forbidden state forever — for this is so even per R' Eliezer, as Tosafot and Rosh in ch. Gid HaNasheh and Shulchan Aruch at the beginning of siman 106 wrote — this is clear. And again I saw in book Alei Zahav in Orach Chaim siman 447 that he challenged this reasoning of Rosh and left it in requires study; in my humble opinion it works out: nevertheless it does not forbid in any amount to the water, for it is not stricter than if now the forbidden wine fell into the water.`,
    '7#_': `(There) And if it fell in the empty place, etc., and if he pours the food the food becomes forbidden, etc. It deals when there is not in the food sixty-one times sixty slightly less against the drop — for if there is in the food sixty-one times sixty it does not forbid if he pours. Nevertheless l'chatchila one should not pour, for one does not nullify issur l'chatchila — and the same for a new pot that it is forbidden to pour l'chatchila. And Peri Chadash wrote: however, if this vessel is not normally used for a small amount but only in abundance, it is permitted to use it, for impossible to reach noten ta'am as below siman 99 and 122 — end of his words, Acharonim see there. And it appears to me that if it fell not opposite the gravy — we say the drop forbids up to sixty against it — if so it is not a slight issur and it is forbidden to use it l'chatchila; and perhaps Peri Chadash deals with falling opposite the gravy and the like. And Magen Avraham wrote that when fallen opposite gravy, for all views the pot is permitted since here there is only a mere concern lest a slight issur remained in the pot — and this is not like the implication of Beit Lechem Yehudah that for Taz and Shach it is forbidden here too, not like Peri Chadash — and it appears he means even opposite gravy, and one may say: and it appears to me that here in any case it is forbidden to pour l'chatchila even if there is sixty-one times sixty, lest he not pour all at once but a little remains at the end of the pouring until there is not sixty-one times sixty and it forbids — as below siman 127 see there. And it appears that if he already poured and does not know if it remained in the pot or if he poured at once, there is reason to forbid — as proven explicitly in Tur and Beit Yosef and Taz and Acharonim there in siman 107 regarding eggs that were cooked and a chick was found in one of them — we are concerned lest he did not pour all at once and the issur remained without sixty — and all the more so here where whatever remains in the pot is issur — investigate. And know that if it fell opposite the gravy, there must be sixty in the food against the drop — but pieces outside the gravy do not combine to nullify the drop, as I wrote in this siman regarding if a drop fell on a piece outside the gravy that pieces in the gravy do not combine to nullify it — the same here — investigate. However they are not comparable, for there there is also a leniency that pieces in the gravy are not forbidden without stirring as I wrote above in seif 2 — the reason that issur absorbed in a piece does not go out from it to absorb in others without gravy, as proven in the Gemara and poskim, as I wrote there above in seif 4. But here it appears that if there is not sixty against the drop the pot becomes issur and forbids other pieces outside the gravy — for a vessel forbids food without gravy, as I wrote siman 105 seif 7 letter 8 see there. And see siman 94 and siman 69.`,
  },
};
